WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.880
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding:&nbsp;– who’s joined us from
the public. I'd particularly like to welcome

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.490
a group of young carers who are joining us
today. It is Young Carers Awareness Day today.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:21.860
And they'll be spending a bit of time with
us at the end of the board meeting, just to

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.130
share with us some of their experiences and
help us learn what we need to do better to

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:30.210
support them.
So welcome, and welcome, everybody. I should

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:37.280
remind everyone that this is a board meeting
in public, not a public meeting, which means

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.930
that we don't take questions during the meeting.
But at the end, when the meeting’s formally

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:46.070
closed, David and I will be available to take
informal questions from members of the public

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:52.110
who have taken the time to listen to us all.
With that, I think we've got apologies of

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:58.190
from Andrew Morris. And Noel, I think. Is
that right?

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.890
&gt;&gt;Speaker: He's running late. He’ll arrive
later.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:05.080
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: He's joining us later [inaudible]
that we need to add in.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:13.830
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: I’ve just got something
from my list. I am now the chair designate

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.290
for the southeast London Integrated Care System.
So I start formally in February.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:21.429
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Congratulations Richard. Thank
you. Are there any comments to the minutes

00:01:21.429 --> 00:01:29.050
from the meeting on 28th&nbsp;November?
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Other than I was here.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:32.880
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Sorry, we missed you, so to
speak, we’ll correct that right away. Excellent.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:41.369
I should add that on item 9 on the agenda
is an NHS England Board item only. So I will

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:46.539
share the meeting up until that point, then
hand over to David to chair that piece. So

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:51.979
with that, David, is there anything you want
to say in your chair’s update?

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:57.470
&gt;&gt;David Prior: No. I'll leave it to later.
Thank you.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:01.390
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: For me all I wanted to do&nbsp;–
I try and make a point of saying what I've

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:08.739
been doing out and about. I've been to three
different, but all rather amazing, clinical

00:02:08.739 --> 00:02:15.190
teams in the last few weeks. I spent a morning
with Oliver Warren, one of our colorectal

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:20.220
surgeons in his operating centre and with
his team in Chelsea and Westminster. A couple

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.650
of weeks ago, I was in Bromley by Bo. The
community centre with Dr Sam Everington and

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:32.880
his team and lived up to their fame and was
fantastic but also slightly different.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.280
Last week I was in&nbsp;– sorry, on Monday,
I was in Nottingham with the catering team.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:44.730
Nottingham has one of the best catering services
in the country. Over 65% of all their produce

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.200
is British sourced and I was with the Love
British Food team learning and one of the

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.680
things they do is their Board tastes the patients’
food very regularly. And it's the first time

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.790
I visited an NHS institution where I had lunch
eating what the patients were eating that

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:04.060
day, and it was really good. So just to keep
that on the record. With that, shall we move

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:08.110
on to maybe the more important clinical items
on your agenda, Simon?

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:12.780
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thanks, Dido. I just mentioned
two things that aren't covered elsewhere on

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:21.370
the agenda. The first is that obviously we
are stepping up action for coronavirus readiness.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:28.850
The NHS is embedded in the cross-government
COBRA process. This detect and assess phase,

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:33.440
so called, is being led by Public Health England
and the Chief Medical Officer under the aegis

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:39.280
of the department of health and social care.
But we're also stepping up NHS readiness and

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:45.410
Keith Willett is our leader on that.
The other thing I was going to mention is

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:54.010
I'm pleased to say that today we are confirming
the appointment of Emily Lawson as our national

00:03:54.010 --> 00:04:01.160
commercial director for the NHS. Emily's done
a fantastic job up until now including in

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.020
terms of bringing together NHS England and
NHS Improvement while also supervising some

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:14.730
of the commercial functions. And so that means
that we will produce some other changes internally,

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:19.910
including bringing together the internal HR
people functions with the NHS facing people

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:26.380
functions that Farina will carry on leading,
but we are grateful to Emily for stepping

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.570
up to this new challenge.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes. Well done. Thank you

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:34.840
and well done and good luck.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: That was it. I think the

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.770
rest&nbsp;– we got the 2020, 2021 planning guidance,
we got current performance, we got lots of

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.650
juicy specialised commissioning topics. So…
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: We’ve got a pretty full

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:42.720
agenda.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Let’s go with it.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.530
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay. So with that, shall
we move on to an update on operational financial

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.310
performance? Amanda, are you going to kick
us off?

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:53.760
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you. So first thing
to say is just obviously the information that

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.420
we're talking about today is previously published
data to this is all in the public domain.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:06.170
So everybody will be aware that&nbsp;– particularly
that it remains very busy. And I think the

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:11.360
things that we do want to highlight as particular
positives though, against the backdrop of,

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.741
you know, being very conscious of how hard
colleagues across the whole of the NHS continue

00:05:16.741 --> 00:05:21.030
to work is just the great progress that we
continue to see around mental health.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:26.740
So that's incredibly encouraging, and in relation
to urgent and emergency care, although, hopefully,

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:31.630
Pauline will talk more about what's going
on around the transformation agenda and urgent

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:36.790
emergency care, I think we would just want
to say I think a particular thank you to Anthony

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:41.590
Marsh, and the work he's led on the creation
of the Ambulance Coordination Centre, which

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:46.910
I think has felt like it's been a very important&nbsp;–
well, I suppose it’s a way of giving us

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.970
real confidence and safety for our patients
over this winter.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:55.210
And then secondly, just a thank you to all
of our colleagues in the NHS who have worked

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:59.730
really hard to make sure that we have been
able to open the additional beds that we talked

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:06.010
about back before Christmas, which are definitely
allowing us I think to begin to see some encouraging

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:11.669
signs in January.
Final thing to say is just we are running

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:16.389
in, of course, to the end of the financial
year now. So we still have a couple of&nbsp;months

00:06:16.389 --> 00:06:22.020
left to really try and bear down on some of
our key priorities around performance, in

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:28.030
particular, as a real encouragement to focus
on doing as much as we can around learning

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:33.350
disabilities, where we have seen great progress,
but we have further to go. And the other area

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:39.390
for particular attention is those patients
who are waiting 52 weeks for elective treatments

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:45.400
or routine care. But we know that the standard
we are all committed to is to have no patients

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:50.660
waiting that long. There still are some so
a real focus on trying to get that down as

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:56.300
much as we can in the next couple of&nbsp;months.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Fantastic. Julian, do you

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:02.180
want to follow on?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: On the financial position,

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:08.240
in month nine forecasts which we have only
just consolidated in the last few days we

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:14.290
are beginning to see&nbsp;– well, I think we
are really seeing a set of the commissioners

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:21.110
and providers now forecasting where they are
going to deviate from plan. It's basically

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:27.600
coming in in line with our expectations. And
our current forecast is that the end of the

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.880
year in aggregate, we think the balance of
commissioners and providers will be about

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.990
£400&nbsp;million off plan. As I think I flagged
last time, you know, bear in mind, that's

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:42.979
like 0.5% against the total spend.
However, you know, although it's not encouraging

00:07:42.979 --> 00:07:47.800
that not everyone is on plan the number of
organisations which are forecasting to be

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:54.630
off plan is roughly half what it was at this
point last year, which I think does show progress

00:07:54.630 --> 00:08:01.009
in delivering the financial trajectories that
were agreed even if there is more work still

00:08:01.009 --> 00:08:06.960
to do. And we are working hard with regional
teams and systems providers and commissioners

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:14.270
to continue to try and improve that position.
We are managing the risk though that poses

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:21.610
to the whole NHS mandate, in part, through
having gone harder this year at our own admin

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:30.770
costs for NHS E and I, and in part because
of slightly lower than originally forecast

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:38.450
drugs spend, which is also a reflection of
a lot of the commercial work that has been

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:44.480
done over the last year, two&nbsp;years by the
NHS E and I team.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:51.529
Worth saying on capital, against the allocation
the Department have made, we are forecasting

00:08:51.529 --> 00:08:58.740
to be within £100&nbsp;million or so of that
allocation and we continue to work with NHS

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:04.970
organisations to make sure that funding provided
is spent and as we’ll come on to maybe an

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:08.439
operational planning guidance, we are also
continued to work on the process to speed

00:09:08.439 --> 00:09:16.170
up the whole approvals between systems, ourselves,
the department and the rest of government.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:20.589
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you. Pauline, would
you?

00:09:20.589 --> 00:09:26.319
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: So just starting off looking
at our headline performance for the month

00:09:26.319 --> 00:09:33.569
of&nbsp;December, emergency department attendances
had increased by 6.5%, and admissions – emergency

00:09:33.569 --> 00:09:40.740
admissions by 2.9%. However, when we reflect
on November's data, the latest data available

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:46.480
to us, again, we see the ongoing trend of
the breakdown in emergency admissions being

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:52.639
chiefly zero length of stay. So that clearly
supports the work that we are doing on same

00:09:52.639 --> 00:09:58.269
day emergency care, where instead of keeping
people in hospital overnight, they're able

00:09:58.269 --> 00:10:03.160
to receive diagnostics at the time of need,
and are able to receive the care they need

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:08.300
and then go home and sleep in their own beds.
The breakdown for November in the increase

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:16.519
was 7.3% for zero length of stay, versus 1.2%
for patients who needed to stay in hospital.

00:10:16.519 --> 00:10:21.610
So I think that's a very welcome sign.
I think the other thing to draw people's attention

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:27.160
to is that whilst we have seen challenges
in delivering the four hour target during

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:36.559
2019, when we look at the overall numbers
on edge, during 2019, we assessed, treated

00:10:36.559 --> 00:10:43.649
and admitted 150,000 more patients than we
did the preceding year. So just for a minute,

00:10:43.649 --> 00:10:48.389
I'd like to draw the board's attention to
some of the work that's being done on the

00:10:48.389 --> 00:10:55.509
wider range of services, but now sort of make
up the emergency care offer.

00:10:55.509 --> 00:11:04.730
Looking first of all at 111. And again, looking
at December, 111 received 1.8&nbsp;million calls

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:13.180
year to date. April to December the actual
increase was 850,000 calls. So 1.8 during

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:20.230
December, but when you look year on year 850
of the difference. And for those calls during

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:26.810
the month&nbsp;of December 111 was able to provide
clinical advice for more than a 50% target.

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:33.920
And interestingly, at the end of&nbsp;October,
beginning&nbsp;November, we launched our community

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:41.620
pharmacy offer and actually on the 29th&nbsp;October,
and between the 29th&nbsp;October and the end

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:47.389
of&nbsp;December, 85,000 patients benefited by
that. And these would have been patients otherwise,

00:11:47.389 --> 00:11:52.950
who would have ended up either going to a
GP service or coming to an emergency department.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:12:00.959
111 booked appointments, the analysis that
we have been able to do now that 111 can walk

00:12:00.959 --> 00:12:09.119
into urgent treatment centres, into GP practices
etc., indicates to us that about 39,000 more

00:12:09.119 --> 00:12:15.110
patients are benefiting by booked appointments,
and that these are patients that were previously

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:21.709
gone to our emergency departments, and indeed
with encounters within the four hour metric.

00:12:21.709 --> 00:12:28.269
And so again, transformation and how we are
counting four&nbsp;hours, not reflecting and the

00:12:28.269 --> 00:12:34.059
overall work that we're doing.
During December and the ambulance service

00:12:34.059 --> 00:12:41.100
received an additional 88,000 calls and during
that month, they were able to maintain a cat

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:48.769
1 performance of seven minutes, 35 seconds,
and which I think is quite remarkable. Ambulance

00:12:48.769 --> 00:12:56.670
services during that month also managed to
reduce the conveyancing rate by 2% to acute

00:12:56.670 --> 00:13:04.019
hospitals, meaning that approximately 16,000
less patients were actually transferred to

00:13:04.019 --> 00:13:10.629
emergency departments, and instead receive
the care they needed out of hospital.

00:13:10.629 --> 00:13:16.079
And we continue to see the growth in same
day emergency care as I refer to Eric Air

00:13:16.079 --> 00:13:26.940
and by the end of&nbsp;December 90% of our hospitals
were providing same day emergency care 12&nbsp;hours

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:34.199
a day. The final sort of stat that I'd like
to draw people's attention to is staff vaccination,

00:13:34.199 --> 00:13:41.779
as far as flu is concerned, and up until the
end&nbsp;of December, which is the latest numbers

00:13:41.779 --> 00:13:49.989
we have we vaccinated 48,000 more stuff than
we had done the previous year. Clearly, we're

00:13:49.989 --> 00:13:55.899
still waiting for the January numbers.
And as in previous&nbsp;years for the month&nbsp;of

00:13:55.899 --> 00:14:01.189
November, December, and now into January,
we've had our operational arrangements in

00:14:01.189 --> 00:14:07.569
place and as Amanda referred to earlier, the
National Ambulance Coordination Centre has

00:14:07.569 --> 00:14:15.989
played a key role in those and we've also
had meetings of the NEP committee which was

00:14:15.989 --> 00:14:21.949
established two&nbsp;years ago. And they've been
very supportive and helpful in making sure

00:14:21.949 --> 00:14:27.480
that we're able to have a good clinical dialogue
on what's taking place around urgent and emergency

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:35.329
care. And again, referring back to Amanda's
comments on beds, from mid-December onwards,

00:14:35.329 --> 00:14:41.230
we've been able to provide 1,000 more beds
than we did the previous year.

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:50.910
I’ll just move on and mention RTT. So as
far as elective care is concerned, when we

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:57.889
reflect on performance year today's we see
that our demand growth for elective care is

00:14:57.889 --> 00:15:05.779
actually very low, as set out in the papers:
0.8%. Interestingly, when we look at the size

00:15:05.779 --> 00:15:13.579
of our total waiting list, we see a decrease
from October to November of 31,000. And year

00:15:13.579 --> 00:15:20.989
to date 2% more patients have had elective
admissions. So clearly, we are able to offer

00:15:20.989 --> 00:15:27.940
more care. And I think the important stat
is completed pathways had increased by 2.9%.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:35.499
So again, linking into that issue. Again,
referring back to Amanda's comments on 52

00:15:35.499 --> 00:15:44.949
weeks, we see a 60% reduction in the number
of patients waiting for care after 52 weeks

00:15:44.949 --> 00:15:51.429
from the peak which was June 2018. So the
actual number and at the end of&nbsp;December

00:15:51.429 --> 00:15:57.899
was 1398.
And in parallel to this work – sorry in

00:15:57.899 --> 00:16:05.100
parallel to the work that the outpatient transformation
programme is now taking on we are continuing

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:12.179
to do the day to day and outpatient practical
work around some of the issues that we have

00:16:12.179 --> 00:16:18.769
talked to you about before: video consultations,
capacity alerts, first contact practitioners,

00:16:18.769 --> 00:16:25.589
etc. I think that's it from me.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Do you want to–?

00:16:25.589 --> 00:16:29.299
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Dido. It’s a question
really for Amanda or – and or Pauline. I

00:16:29.299 --> 00:16:35.639
mean given your experience having been in
the hot end of NHS care for – I won’t

00:16:35.639 --> 00:16:43.629
say how many years it’s been, Pauline, but
some time. I mean we clearly have, per capita,

00:16:43.629 --> 00:16:50.769
fewer beds and fewer doctors than most countries
in Europe or in the US. So you can argue that

00:16:50.769 --> 00:16:58.389
we have a supply problem if you like, a capacity
issue. But equally I think we're all convinced

00:16:58.389 --> 00:17:04.919
that by delivering more care digitally or
outside hospital – I mean where do you think

00:17:04.919 --> 00:17:10.740
– where do you think the balance of the
argument lies between supply and demand?

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:17.920
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: So I think the whole and
urgent and emergency care agenda is to try

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:24.050
and ensure that people receive care where
it's most appropriate. And very often that

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:30.100
means in the patient's home, etc. Very often
it means out of hospital. So the work that

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:36.500
we're doing is clearly focusing the transformation
programmes on trying to avoid the need for

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:43.990
an inpatient attendance, or – sorry – hospital
attendance or an inpatient admission. We are

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:50.501
trying to understand in greater depth why
patients select different services, and we

00:17:50.501 --> 00:17:55.430
are actually doing a piece of work during
January, asking patients in a number of departments

00:17:55.430 --> 00:18:00.150
around the country.
But I think from a capacity point of view,

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:05.190
as well as increasing the capacity and all
of those out of hospital services – and

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:11.640
that's the agenda we're delivering – we
do recognise that we need to be able to maintain

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:18.210
more beds in the system for now, and that's
core to this year's work that the beds that

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:24.540
we’re opening over the winter period we
are hoping to keep in place and throughout

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:31.230
the year. Clearly, we will keep that under
review. And as some of the out of hospital

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:36.900
services do better, there will be less need
for beds. But right now we feel we do need

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:44.000
to increase our bed cohort. And both up until
next winter and then probably further during

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:48.490
the winter period.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I wonder whether – I think

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:55.100
we're all very acutely aware of how hard our
people are working across the country. I wonder

00:18:55.100 --> 00:19:00.220
whether Steven and Ruth you’d like to comment
for your – our clinical staff across the

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:03.600
country, how things are feeling and what we
should be doing to support them?

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:11.030
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Absolutely, and nurses, doctors,
AHP and our non-clinical staff are working

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:17.320
extremely hard as well. Our porters, our ambulance
drivers, whoever they are, they're working

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:23.690
extraordinarily hard. That’s why I'm pleased
to see that we have had an increase of nurses

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:28.620
from this time – this year, compared to
this time last year, and the same with healthcare

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:33.330
support staff, but it's not sufficient. And
that's why the people plan, and the launch

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:36.430
of the people plan soon is going to be really
very important.

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:41.600
And that's why I'm concentrating very hard
on three key things really, which is how we

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:49.410
retain our staff more, how we recruit our
staff, not just here, but also internationally

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:54.660
and how we welcome our colleagues from across
the globe. And I guess the third bit of my

00:19:54.660 --> 00:20:00.480
concentration is about how we increase that
undergraduate supply for the future. It’s

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:06.200
not just about the here and now. But staff
are working very hard. And I'm amazed just

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:11.320
by the sheer professionalism they still work
with under the massive pressure.

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:16.510
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: I would concur with everything
Ruth has said. A few additional points. So

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:22.500
particularly for those staff working in our
emergency departments, and our emergency services,

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:28.900
last summer didn't provide the respite in
quite the same way that previous summers have.

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:35.060
And so they went into this winter, I think
with a relentlessness of pressure that they

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:41.670
have not seen in previous&nbsp;years to the same
extent driven by the demand that Pauline has

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:47.400
noted. And the fact that they managed to see
many more patients within the existing four

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:52.130
hour standard I think is testament to the
hard work. And then the second point is although

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:57.130
the weather has been relatively mild this
winter, the flu season came earlier than last

00:20:57.130 --> 00:21:07.480
year and earlier than many&nbsp;years, in December.
So occurred at the start of the winter period,

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:12.380
rather than last year when it occurred around
this period in winter, so staff also have

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:16.110
to deal with that at the same time.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Richard.

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:21.560
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: We concentrate quite a
lot of these meetings on the urgent and emergency

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:27.800
care for obvious reasons. One question I wanted
to ask around the elective care side is – I’ve

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:32.620
noted the last couple of times we looked at
this. We’re starting to see reduction in

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:42.140
the level of GP referrals. And the extent
to which we – well, what do we understand

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:51.040
about why that's happening?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: Again, that is linked

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:55.631
to some of the transformation work that's
taking place. There are –

00:21:55.631 --> 00:21:59.300
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: So this is – I guess
my question is trying to get to, is this good

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:02.070
reduction in referrals, or not good reduction
in referrals?

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:05.090
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: No, no, I think again,
we're trying to receive care where it's most

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:06.330
appropriate. And very often that means in
the patient's home, etc. Very often it means

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:09.270
out of hospital. So the work that we are doing
means that if patients are referred, that

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:14.860
it's appropriate and that there aren't other
options available, that help patients without

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:20.030
them needing to come to hospital or indeed,
without them needing to see consultant staff

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:24.550
in hospital etc.
And I think that this is starting to reflect

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:28.490
some of the transformation work that's being
done and the other offers that are available.

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:33.110
And that's everything from video consultations
right through to having more diagnostics available,

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:38.650
etc., without needing to come into hospital.
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: So this would be what I

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:42.700
would describe as good reduction. As opposed
to bad, reduction. Okay.

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:46.360
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: Yes.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, we've got a desirable

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:51.820
increase within that minus 0.8% reduction
year to date in GP first referrals. A desirable

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:56.610
increase is obviously, for example, urgent
cancer check‑ups. And those are going up

00:22:56.610 --> 00:23:00.521
by between 10% to 15%. But in aggregate, we're
down by 0.8%, at month eight. And that's because

00:23:00.521 --> 00:23:05.080
of alternative service models, things like
the first contact practitioners in musculoskeletal

00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:08.480
services that have been invested in, in a
number of parts of the country. The other

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:12.500
ways of getting a specialist opinion that
Pauline talked about. And as you will see,

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:17.210
when we get onto the planning guidance conversation
for next year, obviously, a big part of the

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:20.800
pathway redesign is around what we're doing
on outpatients which should have a further

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:23.260
impact in the positive direction you're talking
about.

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:27.070
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: So if you look at the mix
within that fail, the things that we want

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:33.120
to be going up are going up, the things that
we don't want to be going up are not going

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:34.120
up. That was my…
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think broadly that's right.

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:35.670
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: David?
&gt;&gt;David Sloman: So actually I – on a similar

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:41.640
theme, because I was intrigued as to – so
if that's going down, what's going up, in

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:45.500
terms of referrals? Now, what's causing the
overall growth? Because I think there's about

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:56.220
4% or 5% growth in other things. I'm intrigued
in that. And then secondly, I'm, I think,

00:23:56.220 --> 00:24:08.180
the extent to which we are – we do a great
job on understanding RT, A&amp;E, etc. But I'm

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:15.090
struck by the absence of similar data on what
most of our people of our patients and population

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:21.930
actually experience, which is community care.
And I want – you know, whether it be waiting

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:30.770
times whether it's big dispersion, whether
it be the join up between various services,

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:35.370
whether it be mental health as well as physical
health. And I'm just intrigued as to whether

00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:41.670
we think we've got a forward-looking view
on how are the performance in aggregate of

00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:47.230
our community services? And in particular,
what is also driving some of the growth? There

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:49.340
are two questions there.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: David, I'm assuming you mean

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:52.320
primary and community care?
&gt;&gt;David Sloman: I do. Yes. Sorry, I'm using

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.260
that in the broadest sense.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I wonder whether we should

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:56.930
ask Ian to just input first and then go on
to Pauline?

00:24:56.930 --> 00:25:05.790
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I think it’s a great question,
David. We have a relative absence of data

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:10.060
in relation to Community Health Services at
the moment and we're introducing a new community

00:25:10.060 --> 00:25:16.470
services data set in primary care. If I can
just give you the example appointments data,

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:22.800
we introduced, 18&nbsp;months ago, a new NHS Digital
data set, it's not fully complete, and we

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:29.050
have a new process. We've been working with
NHS Digital and the BMA to introduce a much

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:34.230
better data set, which we are discussing as
part of the GP contracts arrangements that

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:44.020
will enable us to get a much clearer weighting.
&gt;&gt;David Sloman: I’m really pleased to hear

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:48.110
that, Ian, because I think sometimes primary
and community care is a little bit of the

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:52.410
poor relation or can come across that way.
And I think one of the great achievements

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:56.990
in the last few&nbsp;years has been shifting money,
much more money into primary care and community

00:25:56.990 --> 00:26:01.950
care. You have an argument about whether it's
enough, but there is only so much to go around.

00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:07.060
And I think, recognising the work that's been
done there, but also starting to understand

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:12.650
the drivers and how we can impact channels
so that people use different channels to access

00:26:12.650 --> 00:26:17.990
different services and how we can start to
help those will only start to be really informed

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:23.780
by better data. And our focus, you know; that's
the other side of it, we have to keep our,

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:28.320
I think really strong focus on that.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So two specifics. So one, the

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:34.550
work on digitising primary care and providing
support. And we agreed, last January, a set

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:42.100
of steps where primary care will be switching
on digital services, making available video

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:48.820
as well as digital appointments contractually
by 2020/21. And that programme is proceeding

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:55.740
apace. And then the second is on the capacity
expansion. And later we'll come on to the

00:26:55.740 --> 00:27:01.000
planning guidance. The critical thing is getting
the investment for the extra staff and then

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:06.150
the whole of the NHS, focusing on helping
primary care networks to be able to get the

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:11.730
additional staff in, in additional roles and
critically the 6,000 extra doctors working

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:15.710
in primary care.
&gt;&gt;David Sloman: And where's the growth in

00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:22.130
referrals outside of GPs?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: It’s some[?]. Clearly,

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:28.900
we still have consultant to consultant referrals
taking place in the system. But we also have

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:34.960
designed some new pathways that are leading
to direct referrals. So, you know, we mentioned

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:40.490
same-day emergency care earlier on: patients
can now be referred via same day emergency

00:27:40.490 --> 00:27:47.800
care for normal sort of consultant referrals,
etc. So some of the new pathways are generating

00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:51.390
some of the referrals as well.
&gt;&gt;David Sloman: So there’s – okay. Alright.

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:54.550
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: But we think appropriately,
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I think you make a good challenge

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:59.380
that we as a Board need to just keep making
sure that we are giving the same degree of

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:04.450
scrutiny and challenge to primary community
care as we have historically focused on, which

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:10.070
is the core. And I think we take that away
and make sure that going forward, we really

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:11.430
are.
Any other comments? Wol.

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:17.000
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: This may be something for the
next session in terms of the planning guidance

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:22.390
for this year, but to return to Ruth’s comments,
and to Steve’s comments around staff and

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:27.510
how hard staff are working. I mean, this is
the same conversation we had a year ago about,

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:33.880
you know, staff working to the max, etc. What
comfort can we get that the people plan will

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:39.120
help to address some of these issues so that
you know, this time – this this coming winter

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:41.090
we won’t have the same conversation? Is
it worth exposing that a bit more?

00:28:41.090 --> 00:28:47.950
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I wonder if, Ruth, do you
want to kick off? You alluded to it but be

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:53.540
worth being a bit more explicit.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Absolutely, thank you. So, Praner,

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:58.390
our Chief People Officer, and across all of
the AOBs we've been working very hard over

00:28:58.390 --> 00:29:04.810
the last year, developing the interim people
plan and the core offer within that. And what

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:10.480
that means for me is that we wanted to make
sure we look after people and we look after

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:15.300
their wellbeing, their health, and their environments
that they're working in a more much more positive

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:21.740
way than just what we have perhaps have been
doing only in pockets within the NHS. There

00:29:21.740 --> 00:29:27.780
are organisations across England that do this
superbly. And what we're wanting to do from

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:34.730
the people offer is to make sure that's consistent
across all specialities, all organisations

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:39.360
and wherever you work, whatever you're doing,
you're getting that superb offer.

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:45.400
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Clearly, I should declare
my conflict as it were, sort of chairing the

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:50.490
work on the people plan. But there are a large
number of people working very hard now in

00:29:50.490 --> 00:29:56.880
turning the interim people plan into concrete
action. It's encouraging to see money behind

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:04.120
that in terms of expanding the domestic supply
for undergraduate nurses, but actually the

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:09.390
most immediate action we can all take is to
treat our existing staff better, which is

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:14.000
what Ruth’s pointing to and I think there
is a momentum building to take that much more

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:17.720
seriously everywhere. Steve.
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: So I understand that treating

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:25.730
our staff better and ensuring that they don't
have things that that irritate and get in

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:30.140
the way of what they're trying to do is really
important. But numbers of staff are equally

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:36.490
important because, of course, if you are working
where you are understaffed, whether our agency

00:30:36.490 --> 00:30:42.130
staff who may not be familiar, as familiar
with permanent staff, is another source of

00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:46.520
stress for staff. So the numbers bit of the
people plan is important, which is why the

00:30:46.520 --> 00:30:53.990
expansion of nurses is so welcome and so necessary,
why the expansion more for the long term that's

00:30:53.990 --> 00:30:59.740
currently undergoing in undergraduate medical
school places is so important and why actually

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:06.120
things like the deployment of additional staff,
26,000 into primary care, because that – those

00:31:06.120 --> 00:31:09.670
are the staff groups, the pharmacists, the
physiotherapists, who will do a lot of the

00:31:09.670 --> 00:31:15.790
preventative work that will actually then
take the strain of our emergency services.

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:20.850
So staff numbers and where they are is really
important in this as well.

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:25.580
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I should say also – maybe
Emily would like to comment on this. We know

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:30.710
we have an obligation as an employer ourselves
in NHS England and NHS Improvement to work

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:35.290
harder on supporting our own staff internally,
and very much now having – as we bring the

00:31:35.290 --> 00:31:38.570
two organisations together, that's the focus
of what going forward, isn't it?

00:31:38.570 --> 00:31:42.399
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: That's right. So we've done
a lot of work with the whole organisation

00:31:42.399 --> 00:31:45.900
to understand from colleagues, what's most
important to them, and what we can do to make

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:49.460
this organisation a place where everybody
can do their best work.

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:52.350
And over the last six&nbsp;months, in particular,
obviously worked very closely with the people

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:55.800
team to make sure this is lined up with the
core offer in the people plan so that everything

00:31:55.800 --> 00:32:02.059
will be coherent. And we had a very productive
session at the Executive last week to review

00:32:02.059 --> 00:32:06.380
both the sort of longer term plan what we're
aiming for, but also some immediate actions

00:32:06.380 --> 00:32:12.200
for the next few&nbsp;months, which the Executive
backed and committed to so we're already doing

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:16.630
a lot of the work in terms of improving some
of our internal processes, reducing bureaucracy,

00:32:16.630 --> 00:32:22.100
but a lot more to do going forward and I know
the HR team and the operating model team are

00:32:22.100 --> 00:32:24.990
keen to press ahead with that with all due
speed.

00:32:24.990 --> 00:32:28.751
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any other – David?
&gt;&gt;David Sloman: Just one other question in

00:32:28.751 --> 00:32:34.390
– there's a fact in the paper, which is
interesting, showing that 85,700 patients

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:42.550
have been redirected by 111 to community pharmacy.
That sounds like a lot of patients but put

00:32:42.550 --> 00:32:45.240
it into perspective. Is it a lot of patients?
What is it…?

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:50.930
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: For a new service we're really
pleased with the initial mobilisation phase.

00:32:50.930 --> 00:33:00.520
in context, there are over 310&nbsp;million appointments
in general practice every year. The previous

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:07.820
estimate is that potentially 20&nbsp;million appointments
could be diverted into community pharmacy.

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:14.420
Our wider plans here around a bigger build
up through the community pharmacy contract.

00:33:14.420 --> 00:33:21.929
The initial work is with 111 and as Pauline
said specifically around diverting patients

00:33:21.929 --> 00:33:28.160
who would have otherwise gone to A&amp;E or to
a GP practice. And at the moment, we have

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:34.030
piloting work, which is coming to a close
of whether we can have an effective diversion

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:42.660
system from general practices, direct to pharmacy,
and to also expand to other settings, including

00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:49.240
diversion from A&amp;E departments to community
pharmacy as part of a fully integrated channel

00:33:49.240 --> 00:33:54.710
management approach.
&gt;&gt;Joanne Shaw: So the thing I’d like to

00:33:54.710 --> 00:34:01.840
build from that is - so we described this
afternoon and a great deal of our focus is

00:34:01.840 --> 00:34:07.040
around what goes on in hospitals and increasingly
we need to and are focusing on primary and

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:12.400
community resources, building those creating
more channels, and so on. I think the bit

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:18.389
that we also need to have clearly within our
sights is the upstream piece from that which

00:34:18.389 --> 00:34:23.879
is supporting patients and families to do
more for themselves by deploying technologies

00:34:23.879 --> 00:34:33.970
and new approaches so that people are empowered,
enabled, supported to make decisions and take

00:34:33.970 --> 00:34:36.419
control of their own health.
And that's something we've talked about for

00:34:36.419 --> 00:34:40.909
a long time and whilst I absolutely welcome
the multi-channel approach to primary care,

00:34:40.909 --> 00:34:47.249
for example, and the – and the video consultations,
actually, when we think about how other sectors

00:34:47.249 --> 00:34:54.499
have modernised – banking wasn't transformed
by having a one on one with your bank manager

00:34:54.499 --> 00:35:01.539
or your bank clerk over the – over the phone
or internet, it was about putting tools into

00:35:01.539 --> 00:35:06.549
people's hands on their devices, their phones,
so they could take control of their money.

00:35:06.549 --> 00:35:12.539
And we need to obviously be exploring very
actively, which aspects of that we can apply

00:35:12.539 --> 00:35:16.849
to people's health. I know we are doing that
and Ian maybe you could say a bit more about

00:35:16.849 --> 00:35:19.839
it.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So we're doing exactly that in

00:35:19.839 --> 00:35:26.960
relation to primary care, including through
the digital work and we discussed I think

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:32.750
in October, what more we can do to support
that work. I'm also reminded that it was almost

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:39.630
exactly a year ago today that we discussed
and launched our approach to personalised

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:46.700
care a really critical part of the long-term
plan, and the NHS comprehensive model of personalised

00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:53.410
care which has six elements to it, including
shared decision making with patients, social

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:58.700
prescribing, where we've been making really
big strides and engaging more and more partners

00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:03.430
behind that. It feels like a big social movement.
We're also funding the social prescribing

00:36:03.430 --> 00:36:12.440
link workers as part of the 26,000 staff and
also personal health budgets, where we are

00:36:12.440 --> 00:36:21.140
significantly ahead of our plans and the mandate
goal set by government. We had about 2,000

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:25.289
patients with personal health budgets four,
five&nbsp;years ago. And the latest numbers are

00:36:25.289 --> 00:36:32.069
over 50,000. And we are on track to deliver
early and we heard a year ago from patients

00:36:32.069 --> 00:36:39.349
for whom that has been transformational.
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: I was really pleased to

00:36:39.349 --> 00:36:44.720
hear that a quality of life metric has been
introduced into cancer care. I just wonder

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:48.569
whether somebody can give us some perspective
on how it worked and what their plans are

00:36:48.569 --> 00:36:52.299
for national rollout because that's really
positive thing to happen, because it's normally

00:36:52.299 --> 00:36:55.910
just been used in clinical trial, but to have
it in clinical care is a good metric to that.

00:36:55.910 --> 00:37:01.880
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: [inaudible]?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: I can’t but I will [inaudible]

00:37:01.880 --> 00:37:06.130
to you afterwards.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Great. In which case, I think

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:07.499
we should move on – Pauline, would you like
to–

00:37:07.499 --> 00:37:12.460
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: Just to say one final
thing to finish: I think we have made references

00:37:12.460 --> 00:37:19.769
to throughout the session. But just absolutely
acknowledge the work of our staff. All of

00:37:19.769 --> 00:37:25.319
our staff, our clinical staff, our managerial
staff, staff who work out of hospital, in

00:37:25.319 --> 00:37:31.650
hospital, etc. And, you know, I think it's
just unbelievable the amount of sort of tireless

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:35.900
work they're putting into all of this. And
I think one of the things that I have noticed

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:42.460
increasingly over the last couple of&nbsp;months
is that the more they are engaged in deciding

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:49.109
what the future looks like, and informed about
those steps, etc., the more they're encouraged

00:37:49.109 --> 00:37:53.450
to give us even more, and I think we are getting
better at doing that, but for now, to really,

00:37:53.450 --> 00:37:57.019
really acknowledge what people are doing.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: We all completely concur and

00:37:57.019 --> 00:38:02.749
agree with you. Thank you for saying it so
beautifully. It's also a very nice segue into

00:38:02.749 --> 00:38:08.529
the looking forward. And the next item on
the agenda, the operational providing and

00:38:08.529 --> 00:38:18.220
contracting guidance for 2021. This is the
first time we've done this as a combined team

00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:24.079
and certainly from inside, NHS England, NHS
Improvement, it's been quite noticeable to

00:38:24.079 --> 00:38:29.440
watch a unified team working on this. So I
just wanted to say well done to the team who

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:35.770
have done that. It also – I think there
is an awful lot in this – in this document.

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:40.349
So I just wonder if – Julian if you're going
to so kick off, if you could just pull out

00:38:40.349 --> 00:38:45.060
the big messages within it that you want us
to focus on?

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:50.259
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So, by the way, our aim is
to publish this today.

00:38:50.259 --> 00:38:53.859
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Right.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: And Amanda may want to say

00:38:53.859 --> 00:39:01.680
some more. I think they're really big messages
here are firstly, a real focus to go to a

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:08.989
bit of the earlier conversation on genuinely,
now, improving the sort of core operational

00:39:08.989 --> 00:39:14.270
performance and making sure that there is
the capacity in particular the bed capacity

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:20.190
and the nursing capacity to go with it. That
will be required over this year; I think Amanda

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:28.410
may well want to say some more.
Secondly is now seeing through the delivery

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:33.269
of the kind of long-term plan goals that systems
have signed up to as they've developed their

00:39:33.269 --> 00:39:39.920
medium-term plans. You know, this is now year
two. And as we've just been talking about,

00:39:39.920 --> 00:39:43.999
we asked, you know – we will continue to
insist on the delivery of the mental health

00:39:43.999 --> 00:39:47.431
investment standard to continue to see the
improvement in mental health performance that

00:39:47.431 --> 00:39:52.369
we've seen, and increase in the kind of workforce
in that area, and seeing through the investment

00:39:52.369 --> 00:40:01.269
in primary and community care, as we grow
that number by about £7&nbsp;billion pounds over

00:40:01.269 --> 00:40:07.670
the next four&nbsp;years, and the GP contract
negotiations that are ongoing, and the increase

00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:12.839
in staff that Ian referred to is actually
critical to that.

00:40:12.839 --> 00:40:23.190
And then we are in here flagging some changes
to the financial architecture, in particular

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:31.549
that where systems and organisations agree
financial trajectories with us, we will provide

00:40:31.549 --> 00:40:39.420
the income to cover all of any agreed deficit.
That means that all organisations if they

00:40:39.420 --> 00:40:46.690
hit their plans will be in financial balance
rather than a system where some of that was

00:40:46.690 --> 00:40:50.950
covered by loans and people paying interest.
So effectively, the holes were sort of getting

00:40:50.950 --> 00:40:57.119
deeper. So, you know, giving the – just
the next bit of help to recapitalise the whole

00:40:57.119 --> 00:41:01.650
system.
Secondly, though, the provision of that support

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:06.829
will be dependent not just upon organisational
performance, but upon system financial performance,

00:41:06.829 --> 00:41:13.339
so 50% – so the received a 50% of that help
will be dependent upon system financial support

00:41:13.339 --> 00:41:19.509
to just continue to encourage and incentivise
commissioners and providers together to make

00:41:19.509 --> 00:41:27.170
sure we are delivering in a financially sustainable
and operationally sustainable plan.

00:41:27.170 --> 00:41:35.729
Thirdly that we will pay that support at the
start of the quarter, so that we actually

00:41:35.729 --> 00:41:39.569
help the cash flow management issues that
Trusts otherwise have where they've kind of

00:41:39.569 --> 00:41:50.190
borrow money and then we give them the cash
at the end. And finally, although the planning

00:41:50.190 --> 00:41:56.630
guidance does not – it is not yet in a position
to set out the detail, we are in ongoing conversation

00:41:56.630 --> 00:42:03.710
with the department and the rest of government
about and how the process of kind of recapitalising

00:42:03.710 --> 00:42:09.200
and creating a financially sustainable platform
for the whole of the NHS – whether we can

00:42:09.200 --> 00:42:19.140
still get to a point where we would be able
to and effectively do a debt for equity swap

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:26.249
on historic debts that have been incurred
where deficits exist, so it's just worth flagging.

00:42:26.249 --> 00:42:31.019
That's not yet fully agreed. But we are still
in the process of discussing that with the

00:42:31.019 --> 00:42:36.309
rest of government. And I don't know if Amanda
wants to say any more on the operational side.

00:42:36.309 --> 00:42:41.359
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you, Julian. Yeah.
So just to build on what Julian said, I think

00:42:41.359 --> 00:42:45.999
particularly the point about the way that
the financial framework is now shifting to

00:42:45.999 --> 00:42:50.789
emphasise the importance of systems. That's
something that is kind of writ large, I think,

00:42:50.789 --> 00:42:56.849
throughout the whole of the planning guidance,
this time round, that we are we using the

00:42:56.849 --> 00:43:01.910
term moving to a sort of system by default
model. So we've talked in the long-term plan

00:43:01.910 --> 00:43:07.589
about all systems becoming ICSs by what would
be the end of next year, so very much seeing

00:43:07.589 --> 00:43:13.990
this year as a kind of transformational year
but at pace, and obvious opportunities there.

00:43:13.990 --> 00:43:18.910
That second point, the link into the conversation
we've just had is an opportunity for systems

00:43:18.910 --> 00:43:23.640
to do some of that more transformational work,
because it's the sort of thing that can’t

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:28.740
be done easily, or indeed sometimes at all
by individual organisations, institutions

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:33.220
working in isolation. But as soon as you start
talking about people coming together within

00:43:33.220 --> 00:43:37.329
systems, some of the opportunity to join up
– exactly what David was challenging us

00:43:37.329 --> 00:43:41.450
to do earlier, that conversation about primary
community care, we’re talking about prevention,

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:45.119
we’re talking about population health, as
well as talking about acute hospitals and

00:43:45.119 --> 00:43:48.799
specialised services.
So that's sort of written throughout the document

00:43:48.799 --> 00:43:54.730
is that shift a system by default, an increasing
focus on collaboration and integrated care.

00:43:54.730 --> 00:44:01.549
And, of course, throughout the document is
the recognition that this is building on the

00:44:01.549 --> 00:44:05.650
long-term plan. So this is all about doing
those things we have just talked about, which

00:44:05.650 --> 00:44:10.479
are about changing the way that we provide
our kind of core business so that we are a

00:44:10.479 --> 00:44:16.769
fit for the future. But then we are also widening
the horizons to focus on some of those other

00:44:16.769 --> 00:44:21.251
things that perhaps haven't traditionally
had the same sort of air time or focus. Saying

00:44:21.251 --> 00:44:25.609
that there are some critical messages and
a planning guidance, also, as Julian says

00:44:25.609 --> 00:44:33.220
about core delivery, so access, quality, money.
On those things, just three things I just

00:44:33.220 --> 00:44:38.109
wanted to call out particularly, we had an
extensive conversation a moment ago about

00:44:38.109 --> 00:44:43.849
urgent emergency care but we are highlighting
in the documentation this point about occupancy.

00:44:43.849 --> 00:44:50.609
So the NHS, we know, all the evidence suggests,
you can't run efficiently if you're running

00:44:50.609 --> 00:44:56.690
much above 92% occupancy within hospitals.
So we're going to have to do something that

00:44:56.690 --> 00:45:01.829
recognises that pressure today, as well as
the sorts of changes Pauline talked about

00:45:01.829 --> 00:45:07.430
that will hopefully take us to a more sustainable
place long term. So a real focus on keeping

00:45:07.430 --> 00:45:12.819
those additional beds that are in the system
now open, as well as doing those things that

00:45:12.819 --> 00:45:19.369
focus on community care or admission avoidance
on length of stay that allow us hopefully

00:45:19.369 --> 00:45:23.799
to find a sustainable way of delivering that
level of occupancy.

00:45:23.799 --> 00:45:29.170
Secondly, on waiting times, again, we just
talked about that in detail, but two things

00:45:29.170 --> 00:45:35.380
just to call out: a big focus in the documentation
about outpatient transformation. So for all

00:45:35.380 --> 00:45:41.410
the reasons that we've rehearsed in detail,
but this year needs I think very much to build

00:45:41.410 --> 00:45:46.089
on those really good early pilots, things
like the first contact practitioners, things

00:45:46.089 --> 00:45:50.869
like the rollout of advice and guidance, and
start to see kind of real implementation at

00:45:50.869 --> 00:45:57.269
scale and pace. The opportunity this year
is probably more about using the capacity

00:45:57.269 --> 00:46:01.769
that's created through that work to bring
forward some of those patients who are currently

00:46:01.769 --> 00:46:06.229
in the queue so that we are reducing waiting
times and reducing the waiting list rather

00:46:06.229 --> 00:46:11.579
than reducing the overall number of appointments.
But over the next five&nbsp;years, clearly, we

00:46:11.579 --> 00:46:15.170
would expect to see that shift.
And then the other thing to call out on waiting

00:46:15.170 --> 00:46:20.509
lists is we are making a point again about
systems working together to use their capacity

00:46:20.509 --> 00:46:27.410
flexibly, so that we're asking people to think
together about how to make sure those really

00:46:27.410 --> 00:46:35.710
long wait patients are catered for well within
that 52 week time scale, and also rolling

00:46:35.710 --> 00:46:40.670
out choice at 26 weeks. So that's the other
kind of key component of making sure that

00:46:40.670 --> 00:46:44.009
we've got people and capacity in the right
places.

00:46:44.009 --> 00:46:48.349
And then finally, the other thing that's writ
large through all of this today is this focus

00:46:48.349 --> 00:46:53.279
on workforce. So – and it is both Steve
and Ruth’s points are completely right,

00:46:53.279 --> 00:46:57.849
written in: it is absolutely about a focus
on recruitment and having the right numbers

00:46:57.849 --> 00:47:02.521
of people in the right places, but it is also
about as being the kind of place to work that

00:47:02.521 --> 00:47:07.829
makes them want to stay. And those two things
have to go hand in hand if we're going to

00:47:07.829 --> 00:47:11.229
be successful.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you. Comments, Michelle?

00:47:11.229 --> 00:47:15.630
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Thank you. Health came
up as a key issue during the general election.

00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:20.630
And obviously has been given significant priority
by this new Government. What’s the simple

00:47:20.630 --> 00:47:25.769
message to the public about the plans that
are in place, and what's the change that they

00:47:25.769 --> 00:47:33.359
will see and experience?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, I think the simple

00:47:33.359 --> 00:47:37.779
message is that to will the end is to will
the means. And the means by which we will

00:47:37.779 --> 00:47:41.690
continue to improve the National Health Service
are by expanding our workforce and tackling

00:47:41.690 --> 00:47:45.410
some of the deep seated workforce pressures
that exist across the service. Hence, the

00:47:45.410 --> 00:47:49.009
fact that we're going to have 50,000 more
nurses; we're up 7,000 whole time equivalents

00:47:49.009 --> 00:47:52.960
over the last year, we're going to be very
specifically focusing in the way that Ruth

00:47:52.960 --> 00:48:00.089
said on further net increases in nursing numbers
during 2020/21. The combination of the extra

00:48:00.089 --> 00:48:03.589
undergraduates who are going to be supported
by the financial package that the government's

00:48:03.589 --> 00:48:08.640
making available. The retention work that
NHS improvement was already leading and a

00:48:08.640 --> 00:48:14.519
beefed up national international recruitment
effort for nursing and other disciplines.

00:48:14.519 --> 00:48:21.060
So more staff, better infrastructure, facilities,
the shorthand for that was 40 hospitals, obviously,

00:48:21.060 --> 00:48:25.950
we need a multiyear capital settlement. That's
a discussion which will be going on between

00:48:25.950 --> 00:48:30.759
now and the spending review, but which is
vital to underpin those ambitions. And then

00:48:30.759 --> 00:48:35.390
what does that mean from the public's point
of view? Well, if we've got 50&nbsp;million more

00:48:35.390 --> 00:48:39.670
appointments in general practice, backed by
26,000 additional primary care professionals

00:48:39.670 --> 00:48:43.589
and 6,000 more doctors in general practice,
that will improve experience, but those are

00:48:43.589 --> 00:48:46.859
all really means to the end of the end is
what we set out in the long term plan.

00:48:46.859 --> 00:48:51.719
So the point about the planning guidance here
is that for most people across the NHS, this

00:48:51.719 --> 00:48:55.710
is really confirmation of the work that they've
been doing over the course of the last year.

00:48:55.710 --> 00:49:00.509
The three things that are perhaps new, as
Amanda and Julian have pointed out, a very

00:49:00.509 --> 00:49:06.410
specific focus now on having extra beds in
the system and open next year, building on

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:11.900
the increases we've seen over the winter.
In order to do that, we need the extra nurses.

00:49:11.900 --> 00:49:15.849
And in order to get that right, we've got
to get financial stability into the system

00:49:15.849 --> 00:49:20.849
where we made good progress over the last
year. But as Julian said, we are restructuring

00:49:20.849 --> 00:49:27.809
trust historic debt and we are writing off
CCG historic debt. So the inputs to get us

00:49:27.809 --> 00:49:33.819
the outputs, the long-term plan improvement,
and the next year being the next period along

00:49:33.819 --> 00:49:36.410
this four-year journey to get the whole package
implemented.

00:49:36.410 --> 00:49:41.799
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you. David?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: This is for you Amanda, or

00:49:41.799 --> 00:49:48.799
for Simon, actually, is that in a way, I think
that this is the where we irreversibly move

00:49:48.799 --> 00:49:56.630
away from the main unit of accountability
being the hospital or the institution to being

00:49:56.630 --> 00:50:03.839
the integrated system, which requires a very
different sort of different style of management,

00:50:03.839 --> 00:50:08.130
collective management locally. I mean, where
– if you're assessing, you know, the across

00:50:08.130 --> 00:50:13.680
the piece of the moment, you know, where are
we in – how confident are you that we have

00:50:13.680 --> 00:50:19.059
the management in place to deliver integrated
care systems across the country?

00:50:19.059 --> 00:50:24.720
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: I mean, I think you're
absolutely right. I think this is absolutely

00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:30.279
the year where systems sort of stop being,
if you like, the kind of exception, where

00:50:30.279 --> 00:50:36.140
we've got a moment 11 of the 42 systems that
have been sort of – got the badge that says

00:50:36.140 --> 00:50:41.979
they're operating as integrated care systems
rather than STPs. And we shift to a place

00:50:41.979 --> 00:50:49.069
where actually we are running the way we work
through systems. And this is this phrase ‘systems

00:50:49.069 --> 00:50:54.229
by default’. So where are we now? Well,
we've clearly got 11, we've reached a level

00:50:54.229 --> 00:50:58.829
of maturity that's being assessed, with the
right leadership, the governance and the support

00:50:58.829 --> 00:51:02.650
in place that’s allowed them to reach that
level of maturity, and we have others that

00:51:02.650 --> 00:51:10.619
are on the journey and there is as ever, quite
a mixed level of variation across those remaining

00:51:10.619 --> 00:51:14.099
systems.
But I think the intention of the system by

00:51:14.099 --> 00:51:19.369
default model is to say, do you know, if we
if we want to help all of the systems get

00:51:19.369 --> 00:51:23.039
there by the end of this year, then we're
going to have to start working differently

00:51:23.039 --> 00:51:27.499
right now. And instead of working through
individual organisations, which almost gives

00:51:27.499 --> 00:51:32.559
people permission not to engage, we've got
to start working in a way that makes the system

00:51:32.559 --> 00:51:38.920
absolutely the route in and through to the
rest of the rest of the NHS. That's what this

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:46.359
sets out. And I think we believe that that
is going to help us to give the right support

00:51:46.359 --> 00:51:50.720
and guidance and input to those systems over
the course of next year to get them there.

00:51:50.720 --> 00:51:57.270
I think what is really helpful about the way
we're describing systems at the moment, though,

00:51:57.270 --> 00:52:03.289
is it does retain the responsibility of the
individual components of the system. So this

00:52:03.289 --> 00:52:10.499
doesn't allow us to kind of lose that granularity
of responsibility and focus within something

00:52:10.499 --> 00:52:16.079
that's just called a system. So the clarity
of being able to say we know what everybody

00:52:16.079 --> 00:52:22.380
is contributing to the success of a bigger
system plan, I think is where we have the

00:52:22.380 --> 00:52:27.930
opportunity to keep that level of specificity
and responsibility and accountability, but

00:52:27.930 --> 00:52:31.369
knit it together in a way that is greater
than the sum of the individual parts.

00:52:31.369 --> 00:52:34.980
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Richard, you wanted to come
in? And then Ruth.

00:52:34.980 --> 00:52:40.690
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: Yes, commending the change
the financial framework that Simon and Julian

00:52:40.690 --> 00:52:45.779
mentioned, we have had the last two or three&nbsp;years
an incredibly punitive financial framework.

00:52:45.779 --> 00:52:51.910
And to be blunt, it hasn't worked. And it's
sort of taken us all I think two or three&nbsp;years

00:52:51.910 --> 00:52:57.369
to get to the point of saying, we need something
more constructive and more forward looking.

00:52:57.369 --> 00:53:02.829
And I think what we're getting now does that;
it takes that burden of trying to deal with

00:53:02.829 --> 00:53:07.559
past issues away from organisations that was
– that was a distraction, and the only thing

00:53:07.559 --> 00:53:14.940
I’d do is just make sure that we really
go at this with a vengeance. You know, this

00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:21.460
is not stopping here. There's quite a lot
more still to be done here. To really create

00:53:21.460 --> 00:53:26.509
a financial framework that is now about how
organisations work together in the future,

00:53:26.509 --> 00:53:32.240
rather than what they've done in the past.
So I think we've done a really good first

00:53:32.240 --> 00:53:35.690
step on that. But there's probably more to
do still.

00:53:35.690 --> 00:53:43.520
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: I like the granularity of the
plan. Because it's been great to talk about,

00:53:43.520 --> 00:53:47.549
it's really important for recruitment and
retention 50,000 extra nurses. But I like

00:53:47.549 --> 00:53:52.489
the fact it’s about improving recruitment,
retention, the specialist cancer nurses even.

00:53:52.489 --> 00:53:56.630
And that's really important that we get into
that detail in order to get that positive

00:53:56.630 --> 00:54:00.819
delivery. And the other thing I'm really pleased
is it does actually confirm the final confirmation

00:54:00.819 --> 00:54:07.299
of the provider allocations for the additional
CPD money. And that is going to be very key

00:54:07.299 --> 00:54:14.130
to ensuring that nurses get their £1,000
every three&nbsp;years to in order to support

00:54:14.130 --> 00:54:17.970
their retention, support their education and
training, so I'm delighted to see that in

00:54:17.970 --> 00:54:20.989
there.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: So as a board, are we happy

00:54:20.989 --> 00:54:26.800
to formally approve the planning guidance?
Excellent, good, which I presume means it

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:34.290
will be published. Good. Okay. If we can move
on to our next agenda item, then, Henrietta,

00:54:34.290 --> 00:54:39.309
will you come and grab a chair. Yes. Or do
you want to come in – is that easier, to

00:54:39.309 --> 00:54:49.289
be in the middle of the discussion? So our
next item is our annual update from the National

00:54:49.289 --> 00:54:52.529
Guardian’s Office. So it's fantastic to
have you with us Henrietta. Thank you. And

00:54:52.529 --> 00:54:57.690
then we're also going to discuss our own internal
freedom to speak up policy. So can I hand

00:54:57.690 --> 00:55:00.259
over to you first?
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Well, thank you very much

00:55:00.259 --> 00:55:04.990
for inviting me to provide an update to the
boards of NHS England and NHS improvement

00:55:04.990 --> 00:55:10.400
and it's a great opportunity to reflect on
the changes since I was here last year. And

00:55:10.400 --> 00:55:14.589
I've been hearing this morning about pressure
on staff, and I think it's really vital that

00:55:14.589 --> 00:55:19.739
workers feel valued and supported, that their
views will be taken into account and used

00:55:19.739 --> 00:55:23.890
for learning and improvement and that's very
much what my role is about.

00:55:23.890 --> 00:55:30.440
So freedom to speak up is a growing social
movement. We've got over 500 trained guardians

00:55:30.440 --> 00:55:36.970
in England, in a broad range of organisation,
and we've actually trained over 920 people,

00:55:36.970 --> 00:55:42.710
including the exec leads, non-execs, and champions
as well. We've got guardians in Trusts and

00:55:42.710 --> 00:55:47.480
Foundation Trusts across England, but also
in the national bodies such as an NHs England

00:55:47.480 --> 00:55:53.940
and Improvement, in primary care organisations
increasingly and in independent sector providers.

00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:58.530
And last week, we published our data report
where we showed that there'd been a 73% increase

00:55:58.530 --> 00:56:03.670
in the cases that have been raised to guardians
from the first year to the second year of

00:56:03.670 --> 00:56:10.480
our data collection. And once again, a percentage
of those cases, 41% were related to bullying

00:56:10.480 --> 00:56:16.489
and harassment, and 29% to patient safety.
We saw that there was a reduction – a welcome

00:56:16.489 --> 00:56:22.440
reduction in the numbers of anonymous cases,
down from 18% to 12%. And we believe that

00:56:22.440 --> 00:56:26.930
that's about a growing confidence in people
sharing their details with the freedom to

00:56:26.930 --> 00:56:30.749
speak up guardian, even if they want to remain
confidential.

00:56:30.749 --> 00:56:35.839
But worryingly, detriment – detriment people
that have experienced or it's been reported,

00:56:35.839 --> 00:56:41.019
is still at 5%. And that's the same as it
was in the first year. So that's something

00:56:41.019 --> 00:56:46.819
that we're really focusing on with our partners.
And today, we published the freedom to speak

00:56:46.819 --> 00:56:53.170
up guardian survey, where we found that 44%
of guardians have no ring-fenced time. We

00:56:53.170 --> 00:56:58.120
think this is really worrying because guardians
carry out an incredibly important role, not

00:56:58.120 --> 00:57:04.529
only reactively when people come to speak
to them, but also proactively in their organisation.

00:57:04.529 --> 00:57:09.450
Guardians feel that they are valued by those
who they support and by their senior leaders,

00:57:09.450 --> 00:57:13.640
but less so by their middle managers, and
we're really interested in that. But overall

00:57:13.640 --> 00:57:18.410
guardians agree that freedom to speak up is
making a difference and that's gone up from

00:57:18.410 --> 00:57:24.890
68% last year to 76% this year. So I think
it's absolutely key that guardians are given

00:57:24.890 --> 00:57:29.709
the time and the support that they need to
do their role effectively. And last year,

00:57:29.709 --> 00:57:35.859
I wrote a letter to all Chief Execs across
England with guardians regarding this, asking

00:57:35.859 --> 00:57:40.069
that they have a conversation with guardians
to ensure that they are getting the support

00:57:40.069 --> 00:57:45.799
that they need. And at the National Guardians’
Office, not only do we provide support and

00:57:45.799 --> 00:57:53.210
guidance for guardians, but we've also negotiated
that all guardians are able to access a 24-hour

00:57:53.210 --> 00:57:57.589
employment helpline – well, it's like an
advice helpline – outside their own employers

00:57:57.589 --> 00:58:01.339
and outside the advice they get from the National
Guardians’ Office so that they've always

00:58:01.339 --> 00:58:06.150
got somewhere to go.
Once again, we've looked at the perceptions

00:58:06.150 --> 00:58:10.759
of freedom to speak up guardians and how that
relates to the ratings as by – done by the

00:58:10.759 --> 00:58:16.430
CQC. And we found that guardians and the Trust's
providers rated outstanding, have much more

00:58:16.430 --> 00:58:22.210
positive perceptions about speaking up culture
than in those rated good, than in those rated

00:58:22.210 --> 00:58:27.089
requires improvement, than in those rated
inadequate. And once again, we found that

00:58:27.089 --> 00:58:32.170
the perceptions of guardians in the national
bodies are on a par with the Trusts and organisations

00:58:32.170 --> 00:58:37.130
that are rated between well – between inadequate
and requires improvement.

00:58:37.130 --> 00:58:42.059
And I think this is really important because
it's something about the culture of all the

00:58:42.059 --> 00:58:48.809
leadership organisations, and how that impacts
on the culture of providers. We've also looked

00:58:48.809 --> 00:58:52.440
at the demographics of guardians and this
was something we looked at particularly last

00:58:52.440 --> 00:58:58.219
year. What we found was that we do have a
higher percentage of guardians reporting that

00:58:58.219 --> 00:59:03.920
they have a disability, which is 20% of guardians
compared to 3% of the workforce, which I think

00:59:03.920 --> 00:59:09.009
is very interesting in itself. But there's
again a lower representation of guardians

00:59:09.009 --> 00:59:14.900
when it comes to the diverse ethnicity that
we see of the NHS workforce. And so we're

00:59:14.900 --> 00:59:20.249
working with the workforce race equality standard
team to get a deeper understanding of this

00:59:20.249 --> 00:59:25.349
and to understand whether it actually impacts
on the ethnicity of workers who are speaking

00:59:25.349 --> 00:59:28.549
up.
I think what I'm saying in conclusion is really

00:59:28.549 --> 00:59:33.609
that leadership is fundamental in fostering
positive speaking up arrangements. And so

00:59:33.609 --> 00:59:38.569
we're working with CQC to strengthen the well-led
inspections. And that's with a particular

00:59:38.569 --> 00:59:44.819
emphasis on ringfence time, and on detriment.
But I've been talking a lot about numbers,

00:59:44.819 --> 00:59:49.630
the stories behind the numbers of what people
find fascinating. So we'll be publishing a

00:59:49.630 --> 00:59:56.089
report called 100 voices, which sets out examples
that guardians are dealing with, and also

00:59:56.089 --> 01:00:02.079
that workers have submitted to us, including
wanting to share their names in those reports.

01:00:02.079 --> 01:00:06.109
And what – alongside our annual report,
which will be laid before Parliament this

01:00:06.109 --> 01:00:09.839
year, we will be sending this to all Board
members. And I recognise it's a bit unusual

01:00:09.839 --> 01:00:13.720
this year that I've come without my annual
report. But because this is being laid before

01:00:13.720 --> 01:00:18.829
parliament, the timing of that is somewhat
out of my hands. But essentially the acid

01:00:18.829 --> 01:00:25.289
test is the view of workers. And I was really
delighted that we published a metric called

01:00:25.289 --> 01:00:30.890
the freedom to speak up index last year, where
we took a subset of questions from the NHS

01:00:30.890 --> 01:00:38.480
staff survey and then we – by creating a
single percentage point we were able to say

01:00:38.480 --> 01:00:42.880
to trusts, this is what your workers are saying.
And because it's the same questions year on

01:00:42.880 --> 01:00:47.890
year that goes to the whole of England, we
can look at trends. And what we've found is

01:00:47.890 --> 01:00:54.979
that 180 trusts improved in their freedom
to speak up index score over the last three&nbsp;years,

01:00:54.979 --> 01:01:01.839
and that there was a six-percentage point
national improvement. We looked at the – who

01:01:01.839 --> 01:01:05.789
was the most improved trust, I really want
to give a shout out to London Ambulance Service

01:01:05.789 --> 01:01:12.099
because they had an 18 percentage point improvement
in the views of their workforce about feeling

01:01:12.099 --> 01:01:18.109
supported, encouraged and secure in raising
concerns over three&nbsp;years.

01:01:18.109 --> 01:01:24.690
We looked at the CQC ratings of the organisations
alongside their freedom speak up index scores.

01:01:24.690 --> 01:01:30.619
And what we saw was at the top, the trusts
who had the highest scores were all either

01:01:30.619 --> 01:01:36.420
good or outstanding in well led and overall;
at the bottom, it's a little bit more complex.

01:01:36.420 --> 01:01:40.339
But I think that's something we're really
interested in. And this data will be going

01:01:40.339 --> 01:01:45.619
on to a new cultural engagement page on Model
Hospital, so that not only we can look at

01:01:45.619 --> 01:01:50.920
new insights into the what the information
which is going to be most useful for organisations,

01:01:50.920 --> 01:01:55.039
but also it means that other – the trust
can actually look to see who's doing really

01:01:55.039 --> 01:01:58.490
well in this and approach them for support
and help.

01:01:58.490 --> 01:02:04.459
We've got a lot of comms activities, including
our annual speak up month and thank you to

01:02:04.459 --> 01:02:09.141
the – all people from NHS England improvement
who supported us in that. We've also got a

01:02:09.141 --> 01:02:15.079
new independent website. And we've got a new
case review arrangement recognising the fact

01:02:15.079 --> 01:02:20.269
that things are very different from where
they were three&nbsp;years ago. This is to enable

01:02:20.269 --> 01:02:24.709
the cases referred to us to be addressed in
a number of ways and to ensure that the voice

01:02:24.709 --> 01:02:31.690
of workers is heard. Today, we've published
seven case review reports, we've made 95 recommendations.

01:02:31.690 --> 01:02:35.579
And we expect that all trusts look at the
recommendations to support their own learning

01:02:35.579 --> 01:02:38.440
and improvement.
But one thing I would say is that the most

01:02:38.440 --> 01:02:43.400
frequent recommendation that we've made is
that the trust policy should be in line with

01:02:43.400 --> 01:02:48.479
the national minimum standard. And I know
that there's a review a revised national policy

01:02:48.479 --> 01:02:52.470
that's going to be coming out, but I think
what really matters is that it requires effective

01:02:52.470 --> 01:02:58.500
implementation. There is no single way to
get effective national implementation. But

01:02:58.500 --> 01:03:05.290
my role is to identify and align all the levers
to promote positive change. And that includes

01:03:05.290 --> 01:03:11.160
the behaviours of all leaders, as described
in the people plan, regulate relievers and

01:03:11.160 --> 01:03:15.609
incentives.
So what's new? We heard earlier about systems

01:03:15.609 --> 01:03:21.359
and joining up the conversation between primary
– secondary care, community care and independent

01:03:21.359 --> 01:03:28.739
sector. And I'm really delighted that NHS
England and Improvement have agreed to making

01:03:28.739 --> 01:03:35.479
our funding recurrent so that we can continue
to work with primary care and regional integration.

01:03:35.479 --> 01:03:43.819
We're working with a number of vanguard organisations
who are in general practice and in local medical

01:03:43.819 --> 01:03:48.499
committee defence primary health care services,
but also working with national leaders in

01:03:48.499 --> 01:03:54.069
general practice, dental, optometry and pharmacy
to describe some models and look at regulatory

01:03:54.069 --> 01:03:57.800
alignment.
We have appointed some regional leads. We've

01:03:57.800 --> 01:04:02.150
got six regional conferences in March and
thank you again to all the people at NHS England

01:04:02.150 --> 01:04:05.849
Improvement who've agreed to speak at those.
But this is really to bring guardians from

01:04:05.849 --> 01:04:11.369
across the patient pathway so they can start
having really helpful conversations about

01:04:11.369 --> 01:04:15.489
looking at those gaps in care.
I'm a GP, as you know. And so those are like

01:04:15.489 --> 01:04:21.019
my everyday happening when I'm seeing patients.
I think one of the things – Dido did ask

01:04:21.019 --> 01:04:25.780
– [inaudible] Harding asked me to be challenging,
so I'm going to be challenging. What I've

01:04:25.780 --> 01:04:32.420
seen is that people are becoming very articulate
in describing compassionate leadership. We

01:04:32.420 --> 01:04:38.239
have the rhetoric here. And we have the lived
experience here. And what I want is to bring

01:04:38.239 --> 01:04:44.309
the lived experience all the way across to
the lovely rhetoric. So my priorities are

01:04:44.309 --> 01:04:49.739
to provide – continue to provide support
for guardians understand the impact of their

01:04:49.739 --> 01:04:56.739
role and develop new insights, to work with
primary care and integration, and to provide

01:04:56.739 --> 01:05:00.420
training for guardians, workers, managers
and leaders.

01:05:00.420 --> 01:05:05.539
And so as members of the board, I would look
for feedback from you about our training when

01:05:05.539 --> 01:05:10.569
I come back here next year, so you can tell
me whether you thought it was any good. And

01:05:10.569 --> 01:05:15.589
as a system, I really want the ambitions of
the people plan to create that joined up approach

01:05:15.589 --> 01:05:23.859
so that speaking up becomes business as usual.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Definitely challenging. Simon,

01:05:23.859 --> 01:05:25.410
you wanted to –
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, it was first of all

01:05:25.410 --> 01:05:29.969
to thank Henrietta very much for her continued
leadership. And as she says, we have re-upped

01:05:29.969 --> 01:05:34.721
our financial contribution, which I think
should provide certainty for the freedom to

01:05:34.721 --> 01:05:38.489
speak up office, but I had two questions actually
arising from what you said Henrietta. So,

01:05:38.489 --> 01:05:44.559
the first was around the lack of BME freedom
to speak up guardians across the NHS. We took

01:05:44.559 --> 01:05:49.959
a view in NHS England Improvement that we
would have – at least a third of our freedom

01:05:49.959 --> 01:05:54.019
speak up guardians will be from a BMP background,
given the differential experience of our black

01:05:54.019 --> 01:05:58.480
and minority ethnic staff here in NHS England
and improvement. Do you think that's something

01:05:58.480 --> 01:06:02.859
that we should be proposing across the NHS
as a whole?

01:06:02.859 --> 01:06:07.829
And secondly, given a number of the recent
concerns that have arisen about the culture

01:06:07.829 --> 01:06:12.630
inside some of our maternity units. Do you
think there is a case for a specific freedom

01:06:12.630 --> 01:06:17.799
to speak up programme aimed at maternity services?
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: So I think freedom speak

01:06:17.799 --> 01:06:23.950
up guardian’s act as the lightning rod of
an organisation. And speaking up is a relational

01:06:23.950 --> 01:06:29.499
exercise. So it only works effectively if
there's listening up as well. What I think

01:06:29.499 --> 01:06:35.199
is really important is that organisations
who don't have guardians or champions that

01:06:35.199 --> 01:06:40.119
reflect the diversity of their workforce,
ask themselves the question, why? Why are

01:06:40.119 --> 01:06:44.160
those staff not coming forward times take
those roles?

01:06:44.160 --> 01:06:48.380
It's a particularly vulnerable role. It's
a particularly vulnerable role for anybody

01:06:48.380 --> 01:06:52.759
but if somebody is already feeling vulnerable,
they need to have the confidence that their

01:06:52.759 --> 01:06:58.609
leadership will be listening and not using
them as the – as the messenger. So that's

01:06:58.609 --> 01:07:02.509
the first thing I would say: it's not about
people not putting them forward. It's about

01:07:02.509 --> 01:07:07.930
the organisation looking very carefully about
their staff survey results and asking their

01:07:07.930 --> 01:07:12.130
BME network or their race equality network,
what is it that stops you coming forward?

01:07:12.130 --> 01:07:16.369
That's what I would say.
The second thing is about maternity. So this

01:07:16.369 --> 01:07:20.569
is the year of the nurse and the midwife,
and I'm really delighted that we have so many

01:07:20.569 --> 01:07:26.099
nurses and midwives who are guardians. We
also know that from our data report that nurses

01:07:26.099 --> 01:07:32.369
make up 30% of the people who are speaking
up. So I do think that there's a – guardians

01:07:32.369 --> 01:07:39.559
need to have the time to be able to provide
support to the entire workforce. And we know

01:07:39.559 --> 01:07:46.220
that guardians who have the time are able
to go out proactively to the closed parts

01:07:46.220 --> 01:07:52.769
of the trust, including labour ward, including
A&amp;E, including theatres, and ITU and sometimes

01:07:52.769 --> 01:07:57.599
including the executive corridor.
But the main thing about it is that they need

01:07:57.599 --> 01:08:01.769
the time to be able to do that. And that's
why I really welcome the fact that the CQC

01:08:01.769 --> 01:08:06.400
are going to focus on ringfenced time. I don't
think we can ask guardians to do more with

01:08:06.400 --> 01:08:13.369
less, if that makes sense. So if there is
a particular inclination for organisations

01:08:13.369 --> 01:08:17.279
to have additional support to maternity, they
need to think about what the guardian needs

01:08:17.279 --> 01:08:21.819
to be able to do that.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Wol, you wanted to come in?

01:08:21.819 --> 01:08:27.330
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: So last year I found your work
rather inspiring. You've challenged the Board.

01:08:27.330 --> 01:08:33.390
And I'm going to sort of flip it back to you
a little bit here by saying, at what point

01:08:33.390 --> 01:08:40.630
– would you perceive the fact that your
organisation is not needed, as being success?

01:08:40.630 --> 01:08:44.960
And when do you think we would get there?
What would be disappointing? If it’s ten&nbsp;years

01:08:44.960 --> 01:08:49.660
out, and you're still doing what you're doing,
is that really bad? Because for me, what you're

01:08:49.660 --> 01:08:55.730
doing is – the basis of the culture is trust
and trust to be able to communicate, and it's

01:08:55.730 --> 01:09:00.520
two way. So I'll play back to you what would
be disappointing for you, so that we can actually

01:09:00.520 --> 01:09:04.240
understand the scale of the challenge.
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: So I had a three-year

01:09:04.240 --> 01:09:15.490
post initially, which has now become permanent.
When I spoke to people in aviation, they said

01:09:15.490 --> 01:09:21.460
they've been doing this for 40&nbsp;years, and
they're not there yet. What when we look at

01:09:21.460 --> 01:09:26.339
the movement in the freedom to speak up index,
that's the best proxy measure I've got at

01:09:26.339 --> 01:09:32.330
the moment: it would take another ten&nbsp;years
to get to a point whereby, you know, the average

01:09:32.330 --> 01:09:36.840
across the country would be it – you know,
at the best that it is now.

01:09:36.840 --> 01:09:42.690
But I don't think that, honestly, the pace
of change that we've done now – I don't

01:09:42.690 --> 01:09:48.270
want that to flag and often what you see is
you see a bit of movement, and then it stalls.

01:09:48.270 --> 01:09:52.520
The main thing is that all organisations and
everyone within them have got to recognise

01:09:52.520 --> 01:09:57.960
the part that they have to play in that: it's
not about me moving 1.5&nbsp;million people forward.

01:09:57.960 --> 01:10:03.290
It's about everybody seeing that that's what's
important for us to be able to deliver compassionate

01:10:03.290 --> 01:10:09.960
care for our patients and our service users.
And it requires everybody to behave slightly

01:10:09.960 --> 01:10:14.370
differently. But I agree, I don't – I don't
think we should have to have them. But if

01:10:14.370 --> 01:10:21.250
you look at all other sectors, whether it's
banking or retail, or oil and gas, mining:

01:10:21.250 --> 01:10:25.470
everybody has some kind of additional channel
for workers to speak up.

01:10:25.470 --> 01:10:28.440
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: I think perhaps the issue is
that people sometimes regard it as a journey

01:10:28.440 --> 01:10:32.900
without a destination. The trouble with that
is that you don't have a sense of moving forward

01:10:32.900 --> 01:10:37.240
and achievement, and I complete understand,
you know, the length of time you have to have

01:10:37.240 --> 01:10:41.390
this because things change, this is change,
conversations develop. But it's just having

01:10:41.390 --> 01:10:45.470
a way of deciding for ourselves whether we're
meeting the challenge you set out in any way

01:10:45.470 --> 01:10:48.850
shape or form. If the index is one way of
doing it, I can see that, but for me it's

01:10:48.850 --> 01:10:52.540
about just embedding it in the people plan,
embedding it in the way that people behave,

01:10:52.540 --> 01:10:57.640
and seeing how the freedom to speak up is
just a foundation for everything else needs

01:10:57.640 --> 01:11:00.470
to happen.
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Absolutely. And I have

01:11:00.470 --> 01:11:07.130
drafted a part to be included into the people
plan and I look forward to seeing the final

01:11:07.130 --> 01:11:11.300
document.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, so first of all, I’d

01:11:11.300 --> 01:11:18.190
just like to bottle your enthusiasm, commitment,
passion and take it into lots of places outside

01:11:18.190 --> 01:11:22.790
of the NHS, never mind in it. I think the
board probably would be interested to know

01:11:22.790 --> 01:11:28.670
that, you know, ten&nbsp;years ago after the financial
crisis, actually seven&nbsp;years ago, the chairs

01:11:28.670 --> 01:11:34.500
of the seven largest banks and building societies
of which I'm the bank building society element,

01:11:34.500 --> 01:11:39.110
set up something called the Banking Standards
Board, which was absolutely aimed at getting

01:11:39.110 --> 01:11:43.790
a consistent measure of culture, and then
tackling a whole series of issues.

01:11:43.790 --> 01:11:48.980
They've done a fabulous job. And the biggest
single issue there is, guess what, speaking

01:11:48.980 --> 01:11:55.630
up, and the correlation between a well led
organisation, speaking up, and listening up

01:11:55.630 --> 01:12:00.740
– you know, you've got two of those and
one of those, you know – is phenomenal and

01:12:00.740 --> 01:12:05.830
the Board might also be interested to know
that Henrietta’s team are an export from

01:12:05.830 --> 01:12:11.370
the NHS because they're actually supporting
one of my large competitors, who have some

01:12:11.370 --> 01:12:18.360
issues in setting up a guardian system within
that organisation, and that is just great.

01:12:18.360 --> 01:12:22.420
And actually I think others will follow that
because it's working is what I'm told by their

01:12:22.420 --> 01:12:26.990
chair. So well done. You asked about challenge:
I thought you were going to ask a different

01:12:26.990 --> 01:12:33.540
challenge, because you made a comment about
that the ALBs were somewhere between the inadequate

01:12:33.540 --> 01:12:40.130
and requires improvement on leading. So therefore,
I think I was going to invite you to offer

01:12:40.130 --> 01:12:46.220
your advice to Simon as the chief executive
of this particular ALB, and the board as to

01:12:46.220 --> 01:12:49.170
what you think we need to do more of.
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Well, I've already had

01:12:49.170 --> 01:12:52.990
that conversation in private.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I’m not sure how I can respond

01:12:52.990 --> 01:13:01.470
to that. Let me [inaudible]. If you've had
the conversation, that's probably good enough.

01:13:01.470 --> 01:13:08.320
But I would be interested in 12&nbsp;months’
time to ask you then – if I’m still here

01:13:08.320 --> 01:13:12.600
– to ask the question, did he listen? But
I'll leave you not to answer that now but

01:13:12.600 --> 01:13:14.920
12&nbsp;months’ time.
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: Can I answer a slightly

01:13:14.920 --> 01:13:20.420
different question about the export side of
things? and I recognised very early on that

01:13:20.420 --> 01:13:24.810
learning from the best in the NHS wasn't going
to be good enough. So I set up a pan-sector

01:13:24.810 --> 01:13:30.690
network where we bring together multiple different
sectors in England – yeah, everyone has

01:13:30.690 --> 01:13:33.980
this challenge. I was delighted to be invited
to speak at the Investment Association on

01:13:33.980 --> 01:13:40.030
the SN and CR regulations. And, you know,
what we've learned is the John Lewis model,

01:13:40.030 --> 01:13:44.340
where they have a newsletter that anyone can
write into, and a manager has to give a written

01:13:44.340 --> 01:13:48.240
response and the staff get to vote on the
credibility of that response. I don't think

01:13:48.240 --> 01:13:52.310
we're there yet.
We learnt about the way that Scottish Energy

01:13:52.310 --> 01:13:57.730
deal with detriment. We've learnt about the
army mediation service, a whole host of other

01:13:57.730 --> 01:14:02.760
fantastic initiatives that are happening across
the system. We do have guardians outside the

01:14:02.760 --> 01:14:07.990
NHS, we have them in educational, we've got
– working with the Old Vic, who have a guardian

01:14:07.990 --> 01:14:13.740
model as well. And I think what we're really
about is about sharing and learning. Nobody's

01:14:13.740 --> 01:14:19.030
got the answer. We've all got a bit of it,
and it's how we bring it all together.

01:14:19.030 --> 01:14:26.690
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Okay, can I just add her so
how much I value personally your advice to

01:14:26.690 --> 01:14:34.320
me and the executive here? You're unfailingly
straightforward and direct about what we do

01:14:34.320 --> 01:14:41.010
need to do differently. And very wise. So
thank you. It's really much, much appreciated.

01:14:41.010 --> 01:14:47.580
And I hope you hear from us that we know that
we've got to change too. That, you know, you're

01:14:47.580 --> 01:14:54.980
so right that unless we shift from the rhetoric,
which I think we have got quite down pat,

01:14:54.980 --> 01:15:00.440
into action as ALBs ourselves, it's impossible
to expect the service we oversee to make the

01:15:00.440 --> 01:15:04.570
changes as well. So can I encourage you to
keep having those private conversations and

01:15:04.570 --> 01:15:08.760
keep pushing us?
With that, Steve, do you want to pick up the

01:15:08.760 --> 01:15:11.930
other items?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: [Inaudible] Henrietta too.

01:15:11.930 --> 01:15:18.240
And Henrietta and I meet on a quarterly basis,
or quarterly, maybe a little bit less frequently

01:15:18.240 --> 01:15:23.061
– and Henrietta and I have worked together
for many&nbsp;years in North Central London, so

01:15:23.061 --> 01:15:25.560
she has challenged me for many&nbsp;years and
she continues to challenge me.

01:15:25.560 --> 01:15:29.870
&gt;&gt;Henrietta Hughes: I hope you say respectfully.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: No, no, you challenge quite

01:15:29.870 --> 01:15:37.570
rightly. And, and I took over as the executive
lead for internal executive support, lead

01:15:37.570 --> 01:15:43.450
to support our guardians for internal freedom
to speak up at the tail end of the summer.

01:15:43.450 --> 01:15:49.180
Parana Lazaar[?] is our is the executive lead
for concerns that are raised from outside

01:15:49.180 --> 01:15:53.820
the organisation.
And really, since the late summer, we set

01:15:53.820 --> 01:15:54.820
about doing a couple of things. And so the
fruition of which you can see today, but I

01:15:54.820 --> 01:15:55.820
think this is really the start of a journey,
and there is much more to be done. And some

01:15:55.820 --> 01:15:56.930
of the things that we've had in our conversation
are doing a couple of things and so the fruition

01:15:56.930 --> 01:16:02.600
of which you can see today, but I think this
is really the start of a journey and there

01:16:02.600 --> 01:16:07.620
is much more to be done. And some of the things
that we've had in our conversations, I think

01:16:07.620 --> 01:16:12.050
some of the things that we need to do so.
So really what we want to do initially was

01:16:12.050 --> 01:16:16.970
to ensure that we brought NHS E&amp;I guardians
that have been set up a little bit differently

01:16:16.970 --> 01:16:25.760
in terms of numbers, support and constitution,
together, and we provided an overall support

01:16:25.760 --> 01:16:30.050
package for them.
And the policy that we have got in front of

01:16:30.050 --> 01:16:36.020
you today provides some of that support. And
I'd like to thank Henrietta and her team because

01:16:36.020 --> 01:16:41.080
we have been in conversations about this with
her and I discussed it with her only at the

01:16:41.080 --> 01:16:46.250
start of the week. I think it's important
to say that what we really want within the

01:16:46.250 --> 01:16:51.730
organisation – and we've heard the comments
about arm’s length bodies, is that guardians

01:16:51.730 --> 01:16:59.830
should be seen within an organisation as having
a welcome legitimate role to resolve issues

01:16:59.830 --> 01:17:06.810
that are brought to them.
And so we don't want in a sense issues and

01:17:06.810 --> 01:17:12.420
concerns to have to be escalated up through
the organisations; we want the organisation

01:17:12.420 --> 01:17:20.550
to give guardians the support that they need
throughout the organisation to get on and

01:17:20.550 --> 01:17:26.490
deal with the concerns that are raised with.
So that's the first thing this is not a substitute

01:17:26.490 --> 01:17:31.130
for guardians being supported. And I think
the sign of an organisation that is listening,

01:17:31.130 --> 01:17:35.620
an organisation that wants to improve, and
that respects its staff is one that fully

01:17:35.620 --> 01:17:40.470
embraces the freedom to speak up guardians
of what they do so that is what success will

01:17:40.470 --> 01:17:42.830
look like.
Having said that, there are circumstances

01:17:42.830 --> 01:17:48.310
where guardians need additional support, where
they have to escalate, and where I know from

01:17:48.310 --> 01:17:53.500
talking to them, they would value that additional
support and some things can't be easily resolved.

01:17:53.500 --> 01:18:00.210
And so this put – brings together a transparent
mechanism for doing that that has been agreed

01:18:00.210 --> 01:18:04.930
with that side and taken to the NHS Executive,
and I should say includes the regions so this

01:18:04.930 --> 01:18:10.730
is not just about NHS E and I central, this
is about regional teams as well.

01:18:10.730 --> 01:18:17.990
So I won't say much more about this, but I
think it lays out a way in which guardians

01:18:17.990 --> 01:18:26.660
or escalation is required. We have a process
for dealing with it. And there are more things

01:18:26.660 --> 01:18:33.410
that the guardians need. And some of these
will undoubtedly be on Henrietta's list. So,

01:18:33.410 --> 01:18:39.121
administrative support is really important,
which is around the time to do this. Feeling

01:18:39.121 --> 01:18:45.890
that they are supported within the organisation.
We will be focusing on that in the next steps.

01:18:45.890 --> 01:18:49.650
Perhaps additional training in particular
areas according to be trained, but there are

01:18:49.650 --> 01:18:56.190
always more things that we can do to support.
We would quite like to go and visit some of

01:18:56.190 --> 01:19:01.390
the Trusts that are really good at this because
I think it is true that many trusts are further

01:19:01.390 --> 01:19:08.350
ahead than we are and Henrietta is already
offered to facilitate that and I will take

01:19:08.350 --> 01:19:14.550
her up on that so that we can do that.
So this is the first step, I think, in bringing

01:19:14.550 --> 01:19:22.010
this together within NHS E and I, but really,
this is fundamentally to offset a cultural

01:19:22.010 --> 01:19:30.240
issue and a culture in which people feel empowered
to speak up where there is a concern, and

01:19:30.240 --> 01:19:38.660
where guardians and others are empowered to
deal with that quickly, responsibly and effectively,

01:19:38.660 --> 01:19:45.480
I think is what we are aiming for.
When I should have said thank you to Kate

01:19:45.480 --> 01:19:50.190
who's here, Tom Grimes who’s also a key
part of the team, unfortunately isn't here

01:19:50.190 --> 01:19:55.770
today. But we can collectively answer some
questions. And on the BME point, we absolutely

01:19:55.770 --> 01:20:02.170
need to ensure that we bring the two – the
guardians and the champions together, between

01:20:02.170 --> 01:20:05.540
the two organisations, that constitutes a
little bit differently that we meet we maintain

01:20:05.540 --> 01:20:12.780
that that appropriate proportion that represent
our staff groups.

01:20:12.780 --> 01:20:21.620
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: It seems like one of Henrietta's,
big recommendations are about ring fenced

01:20:21.620 --> 01:20:22.620
time.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes.

01:20:22.620 --> 01:20:25.690
&gt;&gt;Laura Wade-Gery: Does our policy –
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: No. And that's something

01:20:25.690 --> 01:20:29.151
that I want to take forward. I talked a bit
about – with the guardians about that yesterday.

01:20:29.151 --> 01:20:34.461
And it's not just about the ringfenced time,
it's about the whole package of support. Because

01:20:34.461 --> 01:20:40.350
of course, it's the time to do what, and some
of the administrative work, some of the – I

01:20:40.350 --> 01:20:46.420
think we can manage a little bit differently.
So yes, we will be taking that forward.

01:20:46.420 --> 01:20:52.600
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I was going to ask that question.
I'm very pleased to do an add on. A couple

01:20:52.600 --> 01:20:57.420
of people have used the phrase and probably
uses this as well, which is listening up being

01:20:57.420 --> 01:21:01.850
actually more important to create a culture
that encourages that speaking up, and I wonder

01:21:01.850 --> 01:21:08.430
whether we should take an action for the Board
to have a listening obsession with our own

01:21:08.430 --> 01:21:14.880
guardians at the next board cycle in private
so that the guardians feel they can really

01:21:14.880 --> 01:21:21.300
tell us what it feels like. And set ourselves
the challenge between now and then, we have

01:21:21.300 --> 01:21:24.400
– we can live up to Henrietta's challenge
that they feel they have the time and the

01:21:24.400 --> 01:21:27.980
resources to do what we're asking them.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I'm sure they'd be delighted.

01:21:27.980 --> 01:21:31.260
And I can assure you, they'd probably tell
you exactly what they tell me.

01:21:31.260 --> 01:21:36.960
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I'm sure they will. But I
think it's important that we hear it properly.

01:21:36.960 --> 01:21:39.760
Okay.
So are we happy to approve the policy on that

01:21:39.760 --> 01:21:45.170
basis? Okay. Excellent. Thank you. If we thank
you, Henrietta, and we look forward to seeing

01:21:45.170 --> 01:21:50.670
you formally next year, but informally, probably
tomorrow. Thank you.

01:21:50.670 --> 01:21:55.050
Right, Steve? It's back to you again. The
clinical review of standards.

01:21:55.050 --> 01:22:01.150
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Yes. So the last time I brought
an update on the clinical review of standards

01:22:01.150 --> 01:22:05.810
I think was the September Board, so this is
another update. The paper is there for you

01:22:05.810 --> 01:22:13.900
to read. Just make a few brief points. So
firstly, since last bringing an update to

01:22:13.900 --> 01:22:20.150
the Board, we published an update on the clinic
standard – review of standards websites

01:22:20.150 --> 01:22:25.300
in – at the end&nbsp;October – right at the
end&nbsp;October, which detailed the work that

01:22:25.300 --> 01:22:31.460
we had done since we published the interim
report in March of last year and some of the

01:22:31.460 --> 01:22:37.250
early findings from the field test sites.
Secondly, I would say once again, thank you

01:22:37.250 --> 01:22:43.630
to the 70 organisations who are testing the
standards for us. I don't underestimate the

01:22:43.630 --> 01:22:50.390
amount of work that requires in those sites,
and clearly in busy periods, asking staff

01:22:50.390 --> 01:22:56.190
to engage in testing is an extra ask, but
they have been enthusiastic, they have been

01:22:56.190 --> 01:23:00.170
engaged and we have learned a lot from the
work that they have done. So a big thank you

01:23:00.170 --> 01:23:05.180
to those organisations and the staff in those
organisations.

01:23:05.180 --> 01:23:11.300
So a brief comment on each of the four streams
so on mental health and part of David's question

01:23:11.300 --> 01:23:17.540
earlier on about holding ourselves to account
on performance in in mental health course

01:23:17.540 --> 01:23:22.250
having a set of standards to do that is important.
And of course, in mental health, this largely

01:23:22.250 --> 01:23:26.480
represents a set of new standards. Mental
health was always going to be on a longer

01:23:26.480 --> 01:23:32.190
time frame because the need to align it with
the deployment of resource as part of the

01:23:32.190 --> 01:23:37.190
five year forward view and now the long term
plan, and it remains on a longer time frame

01:23:37.190 --> 01:23:43.480
both in testing and almost certainly, if approved,
in implementation. And therefore, we will

01:23:43.480 --> 01:23:48.500
look to provide final recommendations by April
2021 in mental health.

01:23:48.500 --> 01:23:53.960
In RTT, which is elective care which Pauline’s
talked about earlier, again, we always knew

01:23:53.960 --> 01:23:57.640
that was going to be on a longer testing cycle
because of the nature of the length of that

01:23:57.640 --> 01:24:04.850
pathway and the ability to test it. But it
is also true, as others have hinted in previous

01:24:04.850 --> 01:24:11.550
items, that the reform of the – transformation
of the outpatient service and the outpatient

01:24:11.550 --> 01:24:15.750
offer, and also work that we will be doing
in diagnostics, including some that Mike Richards,

01:24:15.750 --> 01:24:25.290
who's here today, is doing for us will interplay
with that pathway as well the 26 week choice

01:24:25.290 --> 01:24:29.530
work that has also been referred to.
And so because all of those we have decided

01:24:29.530 --> 01:24:35.890
to put the RTT work onto a similar path to
mental health and to look to provide a final

01:24:35.890 --> 01:24:46.000
report by April 2021. For cancer, the faster
diagnosis standard, which is the main recommendation

01:24:46.000 --> 01:24:51.260
was recommended by the task force in cancer
a few&nbsp;years ago. So that has been well discussed.

01:24:51.260 --> 01:24:57.610
And indeed, we put it in the consultation
around the standard contract a month or so

01:24:57.610 --> 01:25:04.790
ago with an intent to introduce the standard
in April and we are on track to do that.

01:25:04.790 --> 01:25:09.330
And then finally, in urgent emergency care.
We are now in the process of testing where

01:25:09.330 --> 01:25:15.840
we are particularly focusing on the testing
of our critical time standards. And those

01:25:15.840 --> 01:25:20.470
are the set of standards that were recommended
last year, which particularly focused on things

01:25:20.470 --> 01:25:26.270
like stroke, and myocardial infarction, heart
attacks and deteriorating patients, including

01:25:26.270 --> 01:25:31.480
patients with sepsis. So, the sickest patients
and the patients where we have perhaps the

01:25:31.480 --> 01:25:38.150
greatest chance to shift the needle on outcomes
in terms of heart – patient outcomes in

01:25:38.150 --> 01:25:42.280
mortality and morbidity.
So, that's the key piece of work that's ongoing

01:25:42.280 --> 01:25:48.660
at the moment. We have very good engagement
with all our stakeholders, colleges, charities,

01:25:48.660 --> 01:25:52.710
patient groups, and I'm also really grateful
to them for all the – what they're doing

01:25:52.710 --> 01:25:58.210
working with us on this and with that, I will
say no more.

01:25:58.210 --> 01:26:01.573
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Excellent. Simon, is there
anything you want to add?

01:26:01.573 --> 01:26:04.050
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: So as Steve says this is
sort of an end&nbsp;of March, sort of spring,

01:26:04.050 --> 01:26:09.600
set of recommendations for half of it and
then the other half as previously planned,

01:26:09.600 --> 01:26:14.690
work continues going into 2020.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any questions or comments

01:26:14.690 --> 01:26:20.771
at this stage? Tim?
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: I just want to say, see in this

01:26:20.771 --> 01:26:28.960
practice – very, very important process,
are we getting the balance of the process

01:26:28.960 --> 01:26:34.880
of developing the standards, right? Do you
feel like the process is getting the buy in

01:26:34.880 --> 01:26:38.720
that is required? We’re going to have standards,
there has to be buy in for those standards.

01:26:38.720 --> 01:26:40.770
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: Yeah.
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: How do you feel about that?

01:26:40.770 --> 01:26:45.270
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: So as I said, we are getting
an awful lot of support from our test sites,

01:26:45.270 --> 01:26:52.630
which are the main people who are of course,
living it every day, but also from the stakeholders

01:26:52.630 --> 01:26:58.750
that we work with. We have an oversight group
across a range of patient groups, Healthwatch,

01:26:58.750 --> 01:27:06.450
Royal Colleges, charities, and also working
groups in each of the areas or groups that

01:27:06.450 --> 01:27:11.010
support each of the areas. So, yes, we have
and I – we are definitely learning from

01:27:11.010 --> 01:27:17.010
the field test sights. So in urgent and emergency
care, as it says in the paper, we have learnt

01:27:17.010 --> 01:27:24.980
a number of things over and above the original
basket of indicators that we proposed. So

01:27:24.980 --> 01:27:32.610
I – it is definitely the case that the field
test work is teaching us additional things

01:27:32.610 --> 01:27:37.670
and our approach, our conclusions are evolving
as a result of that.

01:27:37.670 --> 01:27:45.140
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: Great. One other question is
about pace. So delivery of medical care is

01:27:45.140 --> 01:27:52.160
incredibly complex. There are innumerable
standards you could work on. Are you – do

01:27:52.160 --> 01:28:01.490
you – how do you feel about that? Are we
making progress at a at a pace that you feel

01:28:01.490 --> 01:28:08.300
the public is –in terms of our duty to the
public, are we making enough progress fast

01:28:08.300 --> 01:28:11.080
enough on the development and implementation
of standards?

01:28:11.080 --> 01:28:16.050
&gt;&gt;Steve Powis: So I think on the testing,
we always knew, as I hinted, that the testing

01:28:16.050 --> 01:28:21.460
phases would be different for each of the
four workstreams. Mental health longer because

01:28:21.460 --> 01:28:26.490
of the deployment of additional resource into
mental health, which we've been undertaking

01:28:26.490 --> 01:28:31.110
over the last few&nbsp;years and will continue,
because ultimately, any set of standards is

01:28:31.110 --> 01:28:35.830
there to support a service model. So getting
that service model embedded in mental health

01:28:35.830 --> 01:28:39.830
is key.
For elective care, as I said, the pace we

01:28:39.830 --> 01:28:45.610
have adjusted because of the testing, because
of the length of the pathway and the ability

01:28:45.610 --> 01:28:49.720
to test ig cycles, but also because of the
work that we are doing as part of a long term

01:28:49.720 --> 01:28:54.970
plan or in outpatient transformation. And
increasingly looking at diagnostics.

01:28:54.970 --> 01:29:00.390
Urgent and emergency care we've been testing
since the end&nbsp;May. And as I said, we've moved

01:29:00.390 --> 01:29:07.390
through various phases of that. And I'm pleased
with progress. But again, we of course, are

01:29:07.390 --> 01:29:11.910
asking staff to test during busy periods of
winter as well, which was an important part.

01:29:11.910 --> 01:29:16.100
So yes, in the round. Yes. But I think it
is, as I've said, important to know that not

01:29:16.100 --> 01:29:21.800
– that there's not one single timetable
for all the four components of this and there

01:29:21.800 --> 01:29:28.690
can’t be.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Phillips: Tim, your question about

01:29:28.690 --> 01:29:34.660
staff engagement, etc. And, when I made the
earlier reference to how motivating it is

01:29:34.660 --> 01:29:39.360
for staff to actually be really involved in
something like this. And that was really with

01:29:39.360 --> 01:29:46.010
reference to the 14 month, sorry, 3C sites,
and whereby all of the staff who are working

01:29:46.010 --> 01:29:51.650
in the departments, and indeed, more and more
staff in the hospital, are getting heavily

01:29:51.650 --> 01:29:57.180
involved in the work that we are doing, and
I think they feel engaged, they feel as if

01:29:57.180 --> 01:30:00.900
they're being listened to. Sometimes there's
debates and discussions on various things,

01:30:00.900 --> 01:30:05.920
but I really think that's paying off.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Excellent, thank you very

01:30:05.920 --> 01:30:15.890
much. If we move to the next item on the agenda,
Bill, are you going to come and talk to this?

01:30:15.890 --> 01:30:23.070
So this is an overview to the board on the
process undertaken to access safety and effectiveness

01:30:23.070 --> 01:30:28.781
at each of the nine organisations that received
services from what was the Liverpool Community

01:30:28.781 --> 01:30:36.540
Trust. And this is an action from the Kirkup[?]
report and an NHSI item Strictly speaking.

01:30:36.540 --> 01:30:41.630
Bill, can you talk briefly to the paper?
&gt;&gt;Bill McCarthy: I will do thank you, chair.

01:30:41.630 --> 01:30:48.660
And just to remind people, Bill Kirkup’s
report was into serious failings at Liverpool

01:30:48.660 --> 01:30:54.630
Community Health Services NHS Trust. Just
picking up on some of the conversations I've

01:30:54.630 --> 01:31:00.090
been hearing today – I think David pointed
out the importance of having, you know, paying

01:31:00.090 --> 01:31:06.520
proper attention to community services, and
Henrietta's just talked now about the importance

01:31:06.520 --> 01:31:13.790
of people feeling able, free and encouraged
to speak up when they see things going wrong.

01:31:13.790 --> 01:31:20.960
And this really is a very sad case of how
wrong things can go when those conditions

01:31:20.960 --> 01:31:24.710
aren't in place.
So we should and I will, on behalf of the

01:31:24.710 --> 01:31:31.880
NHS in the Northwest apologise again, to the
people, the patients, families who are affected

01:31:31.880 --> 01:31:40.940
by this. Bill Kirkup’s report was comprehensive.
This is not addressing the full scope of what

01:31:40.940 --> 01:31:48.070
he recommended; it is picking up a single
recommendation, which was that we should review

01:31:48.070 --> 01:31:55.840
after 12&nbsp;months all the services that have
been transferred to new organisations and

01:31:55.840 --> 01:32:03.720
this paper sets out the methodology that we've
seen through and some of the fairly high level

01:32:03.720 --> 01:32:07.110
conclusions.
I think where we are is we've seen improvements

01:32:07.110 --> 01:32:13.930
throughout, so people should feel safer, more
assured when they using community services

01:32:13.930 --> 01:32:20.700
when they're depending upon them in the area
covered by the old Liverpool trust, but we

01:32:20.700 --> 01:32:27.310
should not be complacent at any point. There
is still work to do. There are still risks.

01:32:27.310 --> 01:32:32.821
And particularly, I think we'd highlight the
massive task that Merseycare took on: just

01:32:32.821 --> 01:32:39.500
the volume of staff and the time it will take
to really move that culture through for all

01:32:39.500 --> 01:32:47.000
of those staff groups, to feel really safe,
really assured, able to speak up very freely.

01:32:47.000 --> 01:32:54.880
There are encouraging signs. So in the interviews
we held, in the visits that were undertaken

01:32:54.880 --> 01:33:02.270
by our teams and indeed, looking at the staff
survey reports, we're seeing positive movement

01:33:02.270 --> 01:33:07.740
in all of those areas and that encourages
us. We think we've come to the point where

01:33:07.740 --> 01:33:17.330
rather than just, you know, labelling the
services as the ex-Liverpool community NHS

01:33:17.330 --> 01:33:23.080
Trust services we need now to make the step
on, so they're seen as an integral part of

01:33:23.080 --> 01:33:29.110
the new organisations and therefore we should
make the step that with, you know, all the

01:33:29.110 --> 01:33:33.050
right surveillance, the right oversight, the
right support, nevertheless, we should now

01:33:33.050 --> 01:33:39.950
use our normal quality assurance and oversight
procedures to support the further development

01:33:39.950 --> 01:33:45.280
in those organisations. So the recommendation
is to is to note the report, chair, and to

01:33:45.280 --> 01:33:51.090
support that recommendation to move into a
more normal way of assuring quality.

01:33:51.090 --> 01:33:57.220
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any questions? Roger?
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: This is going to be a very

01:33:57.220 --> 01:34:03.440
specific one and I apologise for that, but
when you go through the process is undertaken

01:34:03.440 --> 01:34:11.910
for each organisation to provide the assurance,
solutions for health I know nothing at all

01:34:11.910 --> 01:34:17.340
about. There are not many ticks in that, in
that row. Now whether – was that based on

01:34:17.340 --> 01:34:23.070
sort of the risk assessment in that area or
was it based on the numbers of staff transferred?

01:34:23.070 --> 01:34:28.310
It just – it just looked a bit odd next
to the other ones. And actually to some extent

01:34:28.310 --> 01:34:31.620
the Virgin Healthcare one as well.
&gt;&gt;Bill McCarthy: It is. It is based on the

01:34:31.620 --> 01:34:37.500
number of staff. So they're only ten staff
transferred around this specific service.

01:34:37.500 --> 01:34:43.920
And rather than do visit and intervention,
we mostly ran that through telephone conversation

01:34:43.920 --> 01:34:48.790
with the staff to ensure that, you know, they
felt they've moved on and that’s summarised

01:34:48.790 --> 01:34:55.400
then in the annexe.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Any other points? Having personally

01:34:55.400 --> 01:35:00.300
visited Merseycare twice in the last 18&nbsp;months
and spent time with staff who transferred

01:35:00.300 --> 01:35:06.220
over, I think, Bill, you've given a very good
summary and we should not underestimate the

01:35:06.220 --> 01:35:13.170
journey that those people are on in healing.
There is an awful lot of work still to do.

01:35:13.170 --> 01:35:17.530
I think the work that particularly Merseycare
are doing on a just and fair culture is extremely

01:35:17.530 --> 01:35:24.280
impressive. But that's not to in any way diminish
the scale of the challenge. And thank you

01:35:24.280 --> 01:35:27.260
for the very sensitive way that you've described
this.

01:35:27.260 --> 01:35:30.780
And I think you're totally right. It's time
for these organisations to be able to move

01:35:30.780 --> 01:35:35.030
on and their people to move on. I'm looking
forward to actually going up with Rosie Cooper

01:35:35.030 --> 01:35:39.590
MP to visit HMP Liverpool quite soon. She
and I are just trying to schedule that to

01:35:39.590 --> 01:35:45.380
continue the focus. And I wouldn't want anyone
to think that the other actions from the Kirkup

01:35:45.380 --> 01:35:50.190
review are forgotten at all; they're not.
We are quite carefully scrutinising each of

01:35:50.190 --> 01:35:57.630
them. Not least the response to Tom Kark QC’s
review which we will encompass in the publication

01:35:57.630 --> 01:36:04.670
of the people plan.
Okay. Thank you very much Bill. So I think

01:36:04.670 --> 01:36:12.881
I get hand over to you, my lord.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: We’ve got two issues to discuss

01:36:12.881 --> 01:36:16.940
under specialised services. I think John,
you're going to kick off, aren't you, supported

01:36:16.940 --> 01:36:22.940
by David Sloman and – good to see you, Mike
Richards again, we served together in a different

01:36:22.940 --> 01:36:27.340
academic different place a few&nbsp;years ago.
So thank you very much for the work you've

01:36:27.340 --> 01:36:33.880
done on cancer services in London.
But can we kick off with you, John, please?

01:36:33.880 --> 01:36:39.570
&gt;&gt;John Stewart: This item covers two distinct,
yet I think linked areas of NHS England's

01:36:39.570 --> 01:36:44.070
direct commissioning responsibilities for
specialised services, firstly an update from

01:36:44.070 --> 01:36:50.470
proposals within London relating to cardiorespiratory
services. And then secondly, proposals relating

01:36:50.470 --> 01:36:56.690
to Children's Cancer services following a
full public consultation last year on new

01:36:56.690 --> 01:37:02.850
draft service specifications. The thing that
links I guess these two areas together is

01:37:02.850 --> 01:37:08.480
the extent to which these services must be
collocated with a range of other paediatric

01:37:08.480 --> 01:37:13.910
services. We will come on to that shortly.
Perhaps David, once we've gone through those

01:37:13.910 --> 01:37:20.090
two items, I might also have the opportunity
to provide a brief update on a third matter,

01:37:20.090 --> 01:37:25.160
that also relates to children. And that's
actions we've taken and set in train recently

01:37:25.160 --> 01:37:31.160
in relation to children's gender identity
services. But if we kick off first of all

01:37:31.160 --> 01:37:34.150
with cardiorespiratory –
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, John, I'm sorry to interrupt.

01:37:34.150 --> 01:37:39.890
I should have mentioned that Amanda has a
conflict of interest because of her role at

01:37:39.890 --> 01:37:44.520
Guy’s and St Thomas's. I think the rest
of the Board of NHS England don't have a conflict.

01:37:44.520 --> 01:37:48.600
Do you have – you -
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Technically, I’m a consultant

01:37:48.600 --> 01:37:50.510
at the Royal Marsden.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ara’s a consultant at the

01:37:50.510 --> 01:37:55.080
Royal Marsden so has a sort of has a sort
of a conflict there as well, but I don't think

01:37:55.080 --> 01:37:58.840
it's so great that you should feel that you
can't talk on this if you wish to.

01:37:58.840 --> 01:38:02.471
&gt;&gt;John Stewart: I shall be very, very brief,
David. I hope the paper is very clear. It's

01:38:02.471 --> 01:38:07.690
probably taken me longer to introduce it than
it would take me to read it so I won't dwell.

01:38:07.690 --> 01:38:11.860
I mean, the very positive things. This is
a follow-up note following the Board decisions

01:38:11.860 --> 01:38:17.220
about the commissioning of CFD services in
November 17, when we were charged to go away,

01:38:17.220 --> 01:38:21.510
to see if we could do some more work to take
the ambition of the Brompton to work with

01:38:21.510 --> 01:38:25.960
Guys and St&nbsp;Thomas's to both implement the
recommendations of the Board decision, but

01:38:25.960 --> 01:38:30.250
also to see if we could do something which
would even move us further towards genuinely

01:38:30.250 --> 01:38:35.790
world class services across the capital, underpinned
in a model which would enable the Brompton

01:38:35.790 --> 01:38:41.210
to continue to exist as an organisation with
that international brand.

01:38:41.210 --> 01:38:45.310
What you will see there is a consensus position
about what the proposal moving forward would

01:38:45.310 --> 01:38:50.650
look like. The headlines are the four bullet
points which are identified in para 3 of the

01:38:50.650 --> 01:38:57.800
paper, which in summary are about support
for a series of estates developments around

01:38:57.800 --> 01:39:03.650
the rebuild of Imperials and the Evelina Children's
Hospital which would enable us to consolidate

01:39:03.650 --> 01:39:09.060
paediatric and adult CHD services within Guys
and St&nbsp;Thomas's on the Westminster Bridge

01:39:09.060 --> 01:39:14.600
site in a way which would be fully compliant
with the commission decisions which have been

01:39:14.600 --> 01:39:17.800
made.
Secondly, to move services from the – from

01:39:17.800 --> 01:39:23.310
the Chelsea site to the Brompton onto those
onto those, those sites within the Evelina

01:39:23.310 --> 01:39:28.990
and the new build hospital, whilst ensuring
continuity of service delivery of both secondary

01:39:28.990 --> 01:39:33.380
and tertiary respiratory and cardiac services
to the residents and patients of Northwest

01:39:33.380 --> 01:39:38.070
London, which has which has been a very important
underpinning piece of work.

01:39:38.070 --> 01:39:42.050
To oversee that we would establish a clinical
transition board that would be jointly chaired

01:39:42.050 --> 01:39:46.720
by the chairs of Imperial and Guys and St
Thomas's, to ensure that during that period

01:39:46.720 --> 01:39:52.120
of transition, we have continuity of service
delivery for patients and residents throughout

01:39:52.120 --> 01:39:55.770
that period of time.
And finally, this would also be underpinned

01:39:55.770 --> 01:40:00.280
by a merger of the Brompton and Guy's and
St&nbsp;Thomas's, which would give a sustainable

01:40:00.280 --> 01:40:04.800
future for the Brompton but also provide that
– as I say that platform for the further

01:40:04.800 --> 01:40:09.720
development of what will be genuinely world
class services to a population beyond the

01:40:09.720 --> 01:40:15.050
immediate vicinity of those hospitals within
London. There is no decision for the Board

01:40:15.050 --> 01:40:19.250
to make today. This is a note – this is
a direction of travel. It's an update to where

01:40:19.250 --> 01:40:24.230
we are but nonetheless, these proposals do
signal a major programme of work which will

01:40:24.230 --> 01:40:28.470
broadly focus in in three areas.
And firstly, it would be the development of

01:40:28.470 --> 01:40:33.430
the of the capital proposals that relate to
the Evelina, Guy's and St Thomas's and Imperial.

01:40:33.430 --> 01:40:38.240
And it will be noted that the imperial capital
developments flames as part of the HP2 programme

01:40:38.240 --> 01:40:44.640
in terms of the building processes. Secondly,
once we have more detail, more flesh on the

01:40:44.640 --> 01:40:48.320
bones, there will be a process of public consultation
that we would need to go through associated

01:40:48.320 --> 01:40:53.100
with the service moves. And thirdly, we would
now charge the Brompton and Guys and St Thomas's

01:40:53.100 --> 01:40:58.420
to work through what the next steps of a potential
merger would look like.

01:40:58.420 --> 01:41:03.570
The final opportunity this does present is
to encourage our universities across London

01:41:03.570 --> 01:41:07.970
to see whether or not they can collectively
take as I say what is an outstandingly good

01:41:07.970 --> 01:41:13.251
platform to an even higher level as we compete
internationally, in terms of the way we develop

01:41:13.251 --> 01:41:18.100
the academic infrastructure that we have.
So that that's the summary of where we're

01:41:18.100 --> 01:41:20.800
at.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Anyone want to comment?

01:41:20.800 --> 01:41:26.610
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think the trusts involved
have come together in a very constructive

01:41:26.610 --> 01:41:34.680
fashion in the last several&nbsp;months, and I
think we are really unlocking a lot of change

01:41:34.680 --> 01:41:40.610
which has been much discussed across London
for a long time now. And out of it could come

01:41:40.610 --> 01:41:46.260
something really good for Northwest London,
as well as for respiratory services and the

01:41:46.260 --> 01:41:53.290
Brompton.
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: I think I should also declare

01:41:53.290 --> 01:42:00.820
that [inaudible]. I just wanted to make the
point and really thank Simon and his leadership

01:42:00.820 --> 01:42:07.950
in doing this, these steps of changes, reconfigurations
are always very difficult, very hostile. He

01:42:07.950 --> 01:42:12.440
single-handedly brought all the stakeholders
together, it was bottom up. And I just wanted

01:42:12.440 --> 01:42:15.679
to put that on the record.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: [inaudible] respiratory side

01:42:15.679 --> 01:42:24.360
of the Brompton. Where does that lie between
Imperial and these – Imperial and Guys and

01:42:24.360 --> 01:42:26.920
St Thomas's?
&gt;&gt;John Stewart: Chairman there are so many

01:42:26.920 --> 01:42:30.320
things we can spend all day –
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: A consensus has happened that

01:42:30.320 --> 01:42:35.720
all of them are very happy and – which is
really what I would like to – including,

01:42:35.720 --> 01:42:41.280
by the way, many of our patient stakeholders
who have been involved in this consultation.

01:42:41.280 --> 01:42:51.120
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Anyone else like to comment
[inaudible]? Excellent. Okay. Right John.

01:42:51.120 --> 01:42:56.230
Move onto the next item.
&gt;&gt;John Stewart: Move on to children's cancer

01:42:56.230 --> 01:43:03.610
services. Okay, so I'll be brief again. So
last summer we launched a consultation on

01:43:03.610 --> 01:43:09.010
new service specifications for actually both
children and teenage and young adults cancer

01:43:09.010 --> 01:43:14.350
services. And actually to support the Board
in considering the outputs of that consultation

01:43:14.350 --> 01:43:21.510
and take decisions on next steps Simon asked
Professor Sir Mike Richards who's here today

01:43:21.510 --> 01:43:26.950
to help us review the consultation responses
and make recommendations to the Board on the

01:43:26.950 --> 01:43:29.880
way forward.
It’s worth saying that Sir Mike's review

01:43:29.880 --> 01:43:35.120
was particularly focused on the issue of whether
or not NHS England commissioned principal

01:43:35.120 --> 01:43:42.150
treatment centres for Children's Cancer must
in future be collocated alongside level 3

01:43:42.150 --> 01:43:48.950
paediatric intensive care services in addition
to a range of other paediatric services. The

01:43:48.950 --> 01:43:53.570
recommendations in the paper, I think, are
very clear. But I’ll hand over to Mike maybe

01:43:53.570 --> 01:43:57.710
to provide a bit of an overview of his report
and those recommendations.

01:43:57.710 --> 01:44:01.360
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Thank you, John, and thank
you and to the board for inviting me here

01:44:01.360 --> 01:44:06.240
to present the report today. You've all seen
it, so I won't go through it in great detail.

01:44:06.240 --> 01:44:12.010
As John's already said I was commissioned
to undertake this review, working alongside

01:44:12.010 --> 01:44:16.360
the work that NHS England was doing, and in
particular, the consultation on Children's

01:44:16.360 --> 01:44:22.270
Cancer. I wasn't looking specifically at the
teenage and young adult cancers. And the key

01:44:22.270 --> 01:44:27.970
issue is whether a principal treatment centre
for children with cancer should be always

01:44:27.970 --> 01:44:35.041
located with a paediatric Intensive Care Unit.
And the way I've gone about this is, first

01:44:35.041 --> 01:44:39.030
of all, I've reviewed all the responses to
the consultation that came in, which ended

01:44:39.030 --> 01:44:45.210
in August; I've talked to a large number of
experts, parents, charities, and others. I've

01:44:45.210 --> 01:44:51.230
also visited all the main sites in London,
which is the most contentious area. But in

01:44:51.230 --> 01:44:54.960
addition to that, I very deliberately went
down to Bristol to see how they did it because

01:44:54.960 --> 01:45:01.000
that's a model of a very integrated service.
And I've also reviewed previous reports that

01:45:01.000 --> 01:45:05.890
are relevant to this.
As you will have seen, I have concluded that

01:45:05.890 --> 01:45:12.110
from now onwards, principal treatment centres
must be collocated with a paediatric Intensive

01:45:12.110 --> 01:45:16.500
Care Unit, and I would just add to that, and
with a range of other specialist Children's

01:45:16.500 --> 01:45:21.390
Services. We often talk about paediatric Intensive
Care Unit as a shorthand: you don't have a

01:45:21.390 --> 01:45:26.290
paediatric Intensive Care Unit unless it's
serving a range of children's services.

01:45:26.290 --> 01:45:36.130
And my conclusion, I may say, was almost unanimously
supported by respondents to the consultation

01:45:36.130 --> 01:45:42.580
and the reasons behind that are, firstly,
to minimise risk to patients and to ensure

01:45:42.580 --> 01:45:48.790
continuity of care, which is very difficult
if the sites are separated and therefore to

01:45:48.790 --> 01:45:56.340
minimise disruption to patient care, due to
that geographical separation of principal

01:45:56.340 --> 01:46:03.650
treatment centre and PICU function.
So that that's the generality. I recognise

01:46:03.650 --> 01:46:10.110
that these recommendations have particular
implications in London, and I'll start with

01:46:10.110 --> 01:46:15.070
the implications around the Royal Marsden
Hospital at Sutton and St George's which,

01:46:15.070 --> 01:46:21.030
as many of you will know, are about eight
miles apart and 40 minutes’ drive apart.

01:46:21.030 --> 01:46:28.020
I do specifically want to say that the clinicians
in each of those two hospitals strive very

01:46:28.020 --> 01:46:32.420
hard to make care for children as safe and
as effective as possible. And there have been

01:46:32.420 --> 01:46:37.520
no recent serious incidents reported.
However, the geographical separation of the

01:46:37.520 --> 01:46:43.950
sites does in my view, pose a risk to safety
and results in children and their parents

01:46:43.950 --> 01:46:48.620
having to go backwards and forwards between
the two sites really quite frequently. And

01:46:48.620 --> 01:46:53.290
I think that in itself has an adverse impact
on patient experience, largely because the

01:46:53.290 --> 01:46:57.360
consultants or the team that they are under
at one side will not always be at the other

01:46:57.360 --> 01:47:02.830
side. So they're moving between teams. And
I don't believe this situation should be allowed

01:47:02.830 --> 01:47:10.340
to continue longer than is absolutely necessary.
In my report, I haven't made a specific recommendation

01:47:10.340 --> 01:47:15.900
regarding a preferred option on – for collocation,
as I believe that's actually for commissioners

01:47:15.900 --> 01:47:21.730
to decide and that was an outside my brief.
I should say that all the trusts that I visited

01:47:21.730 --> 01:47:26.920
in London would be happy to host children's
cancer services if requested by commissioners.

01:47:26.920 --> 01:47:32.150
But having said that, I think there are three
options that really should be considered in

01:47:32.150 --> 01:47:35.880
detail.
One of those is the Evelina London Children's

01:47:35.880 --> 01:47:40.650
Hospital, which is the largest children's
hospital south of the river Thames by quite

01:47:40.650 --> 01:47:46.260
a margin. There's the Royal Marsden itself,
but that would only be viable if full children's

01:47:46.260 --> 01:47:51.130
services with a PICU and the other services
could be collocated alongside it and that

01:47:51.130 --> 01:47:57.240
of course, is a commissioning decision.
And the third option is St George's which

01:47:57.240 --> 01:48:02.590
is the other half of the principal treatment
centre now. Can I just say a word about what

01:48:02.590 --> 01:48:08.820
happens north of the River Thames between
Great Ormond Street and University College

01:48:08.820 --> 01:48:14.280
Hospital. And I think the situation there
is different: there is only a PICU at Great

01:48:14.280 --> 01:48:24.090
Ormond Street and not for children at UCLH.
But the children treated at UCLH are intrinsically

01:48:24.090 --> 01:48:30.200
patients with a very low risk of needing a
PICU. And when I visited there, they went

01:48:30.200 --> 01:48:36.420
through exactly different patient groups and
what the risk of needing PICU is. So for example,

01:48:36.420 --> 01:48:42.360
with CAR-T cell therapy, the risk is about
50%. And it's not just in a PICU, it is that

01:48:42.360 --> 01:48:48.240
you then also may need access to a whole range
of other kinds of specialist services. But

01:48:48.240 --> 01:48:56.340
they look after a different patient group
at UCLH, very rarely needing a transfer to

01:48:56.340 --> 01:48:59.420
a PICU.
In addition to that, the two hospitals work

01:48:59.420 --> 01:49:05.021
very closely together and have frequent multidisciplinary
team meetings between those two sites. That's

01:49:05.021 --> 01:49:09.260
facilitated by the fact that they are the
distance between the two sites is really very

01:49:09.260 --> 01:49:14.860
short. And I looked at transfers between the
two sites, the only ones that really crop

01:49:14.860 --> 01:49:21.150
up are occasionally, patients who are having
radiotherapy need to get to a site with neurosurgery.

01:49:21.150 --> 01:49:27.210
And you can't uproot the whole radiotherapy
service, particularly now that it's got a

01:49:27.210 --> 01:49:33.200
proton beam therapy service, at UCLH. So I
think those two are quite different. So I'm

01:49:33.200 --> 01:49:36.560
making a different recommendation north of
the river from south of the river. And I want

01:49:36.560 --> 01:49:40.150
to stop at that point.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much indeed.

01:49:40.150 --> 01:49:43.020
Any questions for Mike? Ara[?]?
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Thank you, Chair. I have a few

01:49:43.020 --> 01:49:46.630
questions. Good to see you Mike. I haven’t
seen you in a while.

01:49:46.630 --> 01:49:52.160
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Right. Good to see you too.
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: So just for the sake of the board,

01:49:52.160 --> 01:49:59.590
when you were the very distinguished first
Chief Inspector of Hospitals in the NHS, if

01:49:59.590 --> 01:50:05.820
I'm correct, the Royal Marsden scored excellent
in the whole of your tenure as the Chief Inspector

01:50:05.820 --> 01:50:11.170
of Hospitals in terms of its quality of services?
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: The – no. When we first

01:50:11.170 --> 01:50:19.600
inspected there, we actually rated the Fulham
Road site, Chelsea site as outstanding and

01:50:19.600 --> 01:50:24.680
the Sutton site as good. We also did look
specifically there at the children service

01:50:24.680 --> 01:50:29.050
and that was rated good. But that is absolutely
consistent with what I'm saying now, which

01:50:29.050 --> 01:50:34.470
was the caregiving in each of the two locations
I'm not worried about. It is the fact that

01:50:34.470 --> 01:50:39.400
patients have to transfer between the two
and they are different teams. So you could

01:50:39.400 --> 01:50:44.360
point out that that is something that you're
you know, in CQC’s remit at the moment it

01:50:44.360 --> 01:50:47.770
only looks at individual organisations, not
across boundaries.

01:50:47.770 --> 01:50:53.290
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Okay. One other thing which you
very – this bugs me, I'm sure it did to

01:50:53.290 --> 01:50:59.030
you when you were in St Thomas's is – it’s
fantastic. The combination of ICR and the

01:50:59.030 --> 01:51:07.450
Royal Marsden has scored top when the research
excellence framework comes through of any

01:51:07.450 --> 01:51:14.270
cancer – any Cancer Research Centre including
Cambridge, Oxford. And to Imperial too. And

01:51:14.270 --> 01:51:18.550
have you looked at the impact assessment of
this? Because there's no question there's

01:51:18.550 --> 01:51:23.120
a fairly high-quality research happening as
I said four star is where they've always scored

01:51:23.120 --> 01:51:30.700
in the last two reps. Any concerns about that?
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: And I met with the academics

01:51:30.700 --> 01:51:36.841
at the Sutton side and undoubtedly, they made
the case about how beneficial it is to have

01:51:36.841 --> 01:51:45.470
researchers collocated with the clinical services.
I personally think that it's greater importance

01:51:45.470 --> 01:51:50.410
that the clinical services are collocated
for the patient's benefit. I do think that

01:51:50.410 --> 01:51:56.360
then one has to make arrangements that academics
can move between sites, if that's needed,

01:51:56.360 --> 01:51:58.380
but I don't think that's beyond the wit of
man.

01:51:58.380 --> 01:52:03.480
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: One last one which raises Chimera,
which is, sorry, the CAR-T therapy. I mean,

01:52:03.480 --> 01:52:12.510
NHS England’s commissioning report would
suggest that in B cell ALL There is a – I

01:52:12.510 --> 01:52:19.580
think if I'm correct 50 to 30 children a year
would need CAR-T therapy. 50% of those would

01:52:19.580 --> 01:52:24.900
need intensive care services. So we're talking
about seven to 15 children. Do you think within

01:52:24.900 --> 01:52:30.070
the context of that is worthy of moving and
shifting all these services from a centre

01:52:30.070 --> 01:52:36.970
of clinical and academic excellence to a different
site for essentially out of the seven to 15

01:52:36.970 --> 01:52:42.550
nationally, there might be one or two patients?
Why don't we just say, we will no longer commission

01:52:42.550 --> 01:52:47.450
CAR-T therapy at the Royal Marsden?
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: I think any forward-looking

01:52:47.450 --> 01:52:52.360
principal treatment centre needs to be able
to give the full range of children's cancer

01:52:52.360 --> 01:52:56.940
treatments that there are. CAR-T is just one
example of that that I was giving. There are

01:52:56.940 --> 01:53:04.000
other treatments that have a risk of needing
PICU of well over 10% and some that a 20%;

01:53:04.000 --> 01:53:08.840
CAR-T just happens to be the highest at 50%.
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Thank you. Thank you, Mike. Thanks

01:53:08.840 --> 01:53:12.460
for explaining
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Mike. I just wonder

01:53:12.460 --> 01:53:16.840
if you could draw out – you’ve already
done it, but a little bit more the differences

01:53:16.840 --> 01:53:23.520
between the model the role milestones George's
and versus UCLH at Great Ormond Street. So

01:53:23.520 --> 01:53:28.780
I understand the geography is different in
that UCLH Great Ormond Street are closer but

01:53:28.780 --> 01:53:33.440
on the question of case mix, which I think
was the other principal thing that you thought

01:53:33.440 --> 01:53:38.940
and particularly Ara’s comments about forward
facing and thinking about the future, the

01:53:38.940 --> 01:53:46.110
treatments of the future, including CAR-T
therapy. I think you've highlighted the frequency

01:53:46.110 --> 01:53:51.840
of transfers between Great Ormond Street and
between UCLH and Great Ormond Street currently,

01:53:51.840 --> 01:53:57.290
but with the forward-facing view, does that
hold as well in terms of how that service

01:53:57.290 --> 01:54:00.260
works?
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: I mean, they – the children

01:54:00.260 --> 01:54:09.910
who are treated at UCLH are patients with
germ cell tumours, Hodgkin's lymphoma, sarcoma.

01:54:09.910 --> 01:54:15.880
All of which UCLH has particular expertise
in throughout the age range. And that's clinically

01:54:15.880 --> 01:54:21.540
and in terms of pathology and all of that.
And these are also groups which have very,

01:54:21.540 --> 01:54:27.930
very low risk of requiring intensive care.
There are occasional patients with sarcoma

01:54:27.930 --> 01:54:34.320
who have relapsed and who have – need high
dose therapy: they are all electively transferred

01:54:34.320 --> 01:54:42.260
to Great Ormond Street for that treatment.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Any questions for Mike, while

01:54:42.260 --> 01:54:46.250
he’s here? Where do we go from here, then,
John on this?

01:54:46.250 --> 01:54:51.790
&gt;&gt;John Stewart: So, subject to the Board's
agreement, the next steps would be to update

01:54:51.790 --> 01:54:58.500
and amend the service specification in light
of Mike's recommendations. We would do that

01:54:58.500 --> 01:55:05.530
very shortly with a view to that going live
really from 1st April this year. Then there

01:55:05.530 --> 01:55:11.870
is a separate piece of work to be done which
I will now go kind of hand over to David in

01:55:11.870 --> 01:55:17.440
the London region to properly now look at
the three options that Mike has kind of presented

01:55:17.440 --> 01:55:22.430
in his paper about how collocation can be
achieved.

01:55:22.430 --> 01:55:26.860
The recommendation from Mike which I think
we agree with is that that should be done

01:55:26.860 --> 01:55:32.290
as quickly as possible. You suggested the
six-month timeframe. I think the Board paper

01:55:32.290 --> 01:55:39.010
itself is even tougher than that and says
June this year, but I think that's the key

01:55:39.010 --> 01:55:43.300
next step. But in the meantime, obviously
the Marsden will continue to be commissioned

01:55:43.300 --> 01:55:49.860
as principal treatment centre and we've heard
from Mike about safety of service both at

01:55:49.860 --> 01:55:54.730
you know the Marsden and St&nbsp;George's in the
meantime, but we will put a derogation in

01:55:54.730 --> 01:55:59.620
place that means that they continue whilst
we put in place the amended service specification.

01:55:59.620 --> 01:56:08.190
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Are the board in favour of
this? [Inaudible] directors?

01:56:08.190 --> 01:56:16.820
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: We’ve got clear recommendation
from [inaudible] requires consultation on

01:56:16.820 --> 01:56:20.160
the options as John said.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. And Mike, thank

01:56:20.160 --> 01:56:25.070
you very much for doing this work. It's a
real labour of love. Many thanks. Thank you.

01:56:25.070 --> 01:56:30.690
&gt;&gt;John Stewart: So David, if I may just provide
a very brief verbal update on a third matter

01:56:30.690 --> 01:56:37.100
that relates to paediatric specialised services.
And as I mentioned at the beginning, it's

01:56:37.100 --> 01:56:43.380
in connection with children's identity services.
So this is a service that has attracted much

01:56:43.380 --> 01:56:48.630
public debate in recent&nbsp;months. And it's
also a service that I think we need to recognise

01:56:48.630 --> 01:56:53.510
has seen unprecedented growth in referrals
in recent&nbsp;years, in fact, 3000% increase

01:56:53.510 --> 01:57:00.520
in referrals since 2010/11.
Back in 2016, NHS England put in place a new

01:57:00.520 --> 01:57:05.780
service specification for the services and
at that time, we committed to conducting a

01:57:05.780 --> 01:57:13.290
review of the specification and the associated
policies for 2020. So we're now at that point.

01:57:13.290 --> 01:57:19.380
So to support this work, an independent expert
group is being established to recommend whether

01:57:19.380 --> 01:57:25.760
changes may be required to existing clinical
policies that underpin the use of puberty

01:57:25.760 --> 01:57:33.380
suppressants in children, and for use of cross-sex
hormones in children from around the age of

01:57:33.380 --> 01:57:37.220
16&nbsp;years.
I'm delighted to tell the board that Dr Hilary

01:57:37.220 --> 01:57:41.870
Kass, who's a former president of the Royal
College of Paediatrics and Child Health has

01:57:41.870 --> 01:57:47.810
agreed to chair this independent group. The
working group will be made up of 20 members

01:57:47.810 --> 01:57:54.070
from a range of clinical academic backgrounds
and will include members of the public and

01:57:54.070 --> 01:57:59.830
will provide kind of full details on the membership
shortly. To support that group we have also

01:57:59.830 --> 01:58:05.000
asked NICE to conduct a review of the latest
clinical evidence on this matter which can

01:58:05.000 --> 01:58:08.840
feed in.
And then finally on top of all of that, and

01:58:08.840 --> 01:58:13.260
in order support the development of the wider
service specification, we have additionally

01:58:13.260 --> 01:58:19.070
asked NICE to develop a full clinical guideline
on gender dysphoria in children that will

01:58:19.070 --> 01:58:24.300
help amongst other things to identify when
to refer children and young people into specialist

01:58:24.300 --> 01:58:30.180
services. So once both those reviews have
concluded, patients, families, experts and

01:58:30.180 --> 01:58:35.870
other interested parties will be invited to
then comment on any proposed changes to the

01:58:35.870 --> 01:58:42.090
service specification and associated policies.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, John. Any questions

01:58:42.090 --> 01:58:50.650
on that for John? No? Good. Thank you very
much. Back to you Dido.

01:58:50.650 --> 01:58:56.110
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Thank you. So is there any
other business that anyone has further the

01:58:56.110 --> 01:59:03.920
public Board? I have one very small one, which
is just to remind everyone that I think Jessica,

01:59:03.920 --> 01:59:08.950
you're about to commence our internal annual
board review, is that correct? So you'll be

01:59:08.950 --> 01:59:13.840
making contact with everybody so that there'll
be a questionnaire to fill in but also some

01:59:13.840 --> 01:59:18.340
interviews that Jessica is going to conduct
so that we live up to our commitment to review

01:59:18.340 --> 01:59:24.010
our joint working processes 12&nbsp;months into
working together. That’s it. In which case,

01:59:24.010 --> 01:59:28.190
I think we'll close the formal meeting. David
and I are available for any informal questions

01:59:28.190 --> 01:59:34.330
from anyone from the public, and if I could
encourage the board to stay to have a chat

01:59:34.330 --> 01:59:36.960
with our young carers.
Thank you very much.

